./runs/20261016T025435190699Z/logs/editorial-select_items-sh-1bb39f96d56842fb9a97485ea497c5a7-attempt-2.events.jsonl:4:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151286\",\"version_id\":\"a393edb1af792a0f22f58a9c9be7640474dbeb4ed41d7ec336f5428b607cf2bc\",\"decision\":\"defer\",\"reason\":\"This is relevant commercial CAD and PLM context, but the central capability claims are Autodesk executives’ forward-looking statements: agentic PLM is planned for the next couple of quarters and productivity gains are asserted rather than measured. The interview supplies a dated, attributable passage, but no released workflow, settings, success rate, error analysis, or independently checkable result. It is also coverage of the same AU announcement discussed by Engineering.com elsewhere in this batch, not independent corroboration.\",\"piece_ids\":[]}\n{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151329\",\"version_id\":\"a5a6e64fd4f2a87b5b45be5afd0c01d19fd3ee1e8b8f12be9e7e49964e03d82b\",\"decision\":\"defer\",\"reason\":\"The Comsol MCP server and SimScale-on-Onshape agent are relevant to CAD-to-simulation workflows, but the item reports vendor announcements and a press-release description rather than an observed evaluation. In particular, the claim that one prompt can repair geometry, choose setup, mesh, run physics, and report results names no test models, solver settings, verification procedure, or failure rate. It also continues Engineering.com’s AU coverage, so it cannot independently corroborate Autodesk-related claims in the preceding item.\",\"piece_ids\":[]}\n{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563\",\"version_id\":\"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803\",\"decision\":\"accept\",\"reason\":\"This is a fresh, directly relevant practitioner test of drawing-to-CAD, with a stated procedure: the author imported downloaded STEP outputs into Fusion, applied a specified material, and checked mass against TooTallToby reference exercises. The result supports a narrow claim—two simple single-part drawings passed that mass check while a seven-body shelf failed—not general manufacturing accuracy, feature-tree fidelity, or performance on real shop drawings. The source is independent of Backflip, although the test remains small and should be framed as evidence to reproduce rather than broad validation.\",\"piece_ids\":[\"01\",\"02\",\"03\"]}\n{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/ai-generated-thumbnail-description-search\",\"version_id\":\"b2755a997c3cdda71421bbdd40ee9721d353cbc20ed2f1f80e91169757b9bbd2\",\"decision\":\"accept\",\"reason\":\"The dated vendor post provides a checkable product-scope claim: an opt-in Onshape Labs feature generates searchable text from thumbnails, and it currently applies only to public documents. That is relevant CAD/PDM context for discoverability and reuse, but claims that it will reduce duplicate designs or work across internal and supplier data are explicitly speculative and are not evidence of those outcomes. As Onshape is the feature producer, this is commercial-announcement perspective rather than independent performance validation.\",\"piece_ids\":[\"01\",\"03\"]}\n{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/featurescript-mcp-server-use-cases-text-code-cad\",\"version_id\":\"9656a0a35f95ac0e0adb6ba1530e2759342633cbcecba8de9948699a032ab5a8\",\"decision\":\"defer\",\"reason\":\"The FeatureScript MCP server is relevant to text-to-code-to-CAD workflows, but this vendor use-case article provides hypothetical examples rather than a check of generated features. Its assertions about preserving manufacturing accuracy, generating standards-compliant geometry, refactoring models, and integrating analysis omit prompts, generated code, test cases, review results, and failure modes. A released example with inspectable FeatureScript and geometric or standards validation would settle the promising claims.\",\"piece_ids\":[]}\n{\"item_id\":\"podcast:173f6c2b-e1b8-4de0-b7b7-4b15658d52e6\",\"version_id\":\"da6be0ed10587e503de7706e1ad94cd3d0409d0c4664a004a631d6f27c500a73\",\"decision\":\"reject\",\"reason\":\"Although fresh and timestamped, this episode concerns narrative intelligence, coaching, and human identity rather than 3D modeling, CAD, geometry, simulation-ready assets, or physical-system verification. It offers personal perspectives rather than checkable capability evidence. It therefore fails the publication’s relevance test.\",\"piece_ids\":[]}\n{\"item_id\":\"podcast:509842f7-f592-432c-b01a-ad26a2ae1e31\",\"version_id\":\"122e3cc3de2a41fbd9f0de08a089614db628a34697f00a720e1d3abf5b405dca\",\"decision\":\"reject\",\"reason\":\"This fresh, timestamped discussion concerns AI and education broadly, not frontier 3D modeling, CAD, or robotics evidence that bears on geometry, simulation, or physical verification. Mentions of mechanical engineering are incidental and do not establish a relevant development. The episode is therefore outside the stated relevance scope.\",\"piece_ids\":[]}\n{\"item_id\":\"podcast:b836fa1b-9246-43d5-8cbb-cf1446760a6d\",\"version_id\":\"a0beee0f49195b6f0b08b75af793b6f6acaf56a159b58771b1c5d8f6c294b282\",\"decision\":\"reject\",\"reason\":\"The episode is relevant robotics context, but its physical-AI claims are presented in an interview with an NVIDIA vice president and lack a disclosed experiment, evaluation protocol, or independent replication in the supplied passages. Timestamped automatic-transcript statements about open models and world simulation are source claims, not verification of robot performance. It would add vendor perspective without a checkable result for the audience to assess.\",\"piece_ids\":[]}\n{\"item_id\":\"podcast:https://physicalaipodcast.co/episodes/008\",\"version_id\":\"1cb4eefeb597341d29e650ad7c122c9eea10b1aa146aef9c4a6214acdf2c2983\",\"decision\":\"defer\",\"reason\":\"This is promising robotics context because it distinguishes an NVIDIA simulation leaderboard from a claimed blind physical-arm evaluation and names conditions such as ten tasks, three attempts, a shared scoring sheet, and a blinded operator. However, the supplied evidence is an automatic transcript, so its numerical and technical details remain unverified until checked against the audio and, ideally, the underlying evaluation video or report. The reported external evaluation was commissioned by Black Forest Labs, so even if confirmed it is not fully independent corroboration.\",\"piece_ids\":[]}\n{\"item_id\":\"podcast:https://physicalaipodcast.co/episodes/009\",\"version_id\":\"13aaf6bfc525bce0b9a1887162b0c0c3d4dd024b9bafd1c223a5bc48bd063692\",\"decision\":\"defer\",\"reason\":\"The episode is relevant supporting robotics evidence because it reports a side-by-side world-action-model comparison, flags simulation limits, and says code and weights are available. But the claimed 70%, 6%, and 10% results, task definition, and real-robot scope are supplied through an automatic transcript and have not been checked against the audio or the primary paper and repository. Primary-source verification is needed before treating its reported generalization gap as evidence rather than a podcast summary.\",\"piece_ids\":[]}"}}
