./runs/20261016T025435190699Z/logs/content-write_video_scripts-sh-6f8e33dd3f544acba3fb3cc780cbb453-attempt-1.events.jsonl:4:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"piece_id\":\"03\",\"markdown\":\"## Scene 1 — The answer\\n\\n**Narration:** What do recent CAD-AI developments mean for engineering leaders? The answer is: AI can help with bounded drafting and search. But the near-term implementation problem is verification and workflow governance, not generation. This is an AI-generated presentation. As of October 16, 2026, the practical question is not whether an assistant can finish a demo. It is whether your team can catch a failed result before it changes a design, purchase, or manufacturing instruction.\\n\\n**Visual:** Title: “From CAD Generation to Governed Verification.” Split screen: “Can generate a file” versus “Can authorize an engineering change.” Highlight the second phrase.\\n\\n**Source on screen:** Agentic 3D, “From CAD Generation to Governed Verification,” October 16, 2026.\\n\\n## Scene 2 — A check can support a narrow claim\\n\\n**Narration:** Start with a reported fact from Engineering.com. A practitioner tested two Backflip drawing-to-CAD outputs. They imported each STEP file into Fusion, applied the specified material, and checked mass. Both matched exactly.\\n\\nThat is useful evidence. It supports mass agreement for those two drawings under those conditions. It does not establish every property of the part.\\n\\n**Visual:** Diagram labeled “What was checked.” Drawing → Backflip output → STEP file → Fusion → specified material → mass check → “2 of 2 matched.” Beside it, a muted box: “Not checked: tolerances, manufacturability.”\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 03-c1 — Engineering.com, “Welcome to the vibe-CAD era,” September 30, 2026.\\n\\n## Scene 3 — The core test\\n\\n**Narration:** Here is the core test: a capability claim holds only as far as the check that observed it.\\n\\nThis is an interpretation based on the evidence. The two mass checks establish mass agreement, not tolerance conformance or manufacturability. Engineering.com notes that the drawings lacked the difficulty of real shop drawings. The MIT Computational Design and Fabrication Group survey says tolerance specifications and manufacturability have not been tested on physical parts. It also says benchmark results characterize a complete model-and-harness system, not a model in isolation.\\n\\n**Visual:** A ladder with one checked rung: “Mass.” Above it, unchecked rungs: “Dimensions and tolerances,” “Design intent,” “Process planning,” “Manufacturability.” Caption: “A passed lower rung does not validate the higher ones.”\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 03-c2 — Engineering.com, “Welcome to the vibe-CAD era,” September 30, 2026; MIT Computational Design and Fabrication Group, “On the Opportunities and Risks of Frontier Models for 3D Modeling, Computational Design and Robotics,” September 22, 2026.\\n\\n## Scene 4 — Govern connected agents\\n\\n**Narration:** Connected CAD systems make this more important. Cadalyst reports that Autodesk is connecting applications, cloud data, and AI services across engineering workflows. Some capabilities are in beta or Tech Preview.\\n\\nThe article raises a governance question: who can initiate a change, and who can approve the result? My interpretation is that leaders should assign explicit permissions and human approval gates before an agent prepares or initiates engineering changes. Let the assistant prepare a proposal. Name the person authorized to release it.\\n\\n**Visual:** Workflow gate diagram: AI assistant → “Prepare proposal” → human approval gate → “Initiate approved change.” A red stop icon before the approval gate. Small label: “Some Autodesk capabilities: beta / Tech Preview.”\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 03-c3 — Cadalyst, “Autodesk University 2026: What the Announcements Mean for CAD Teams,” September 25, 2026.\\n\\n## Scene 5 — Retrieval has limits too\\n\\n**Narration:** Onshape provides a narrower example. Its AI Descriptive Image Search is an opt-in Onshape Labs feature, and it currently applies to public documents only.\\n\\nThis is a source-reported fact, not proof that search improves reuse on private engineering data. If you test this kind of feature, check the search result against your own data, naming practices, and approval status before reuse.\\n\\n**Visual:** Search bar: “red bracket.” Results appear under a banner reading “Public documents only.” Then a review card: “Check: correct part? approved status? applicable context?”\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 03-c4 — Onshape, “Introducing AI Descriptive Image Search from Onshape Labs,” October 5, 2026.\\n\\n## Scene 6 — Simulation is a verification ladder\\n\\n**Narration:** Nvidia’s CAD-to-simulation walkthrough shows a more explicit sequence. First, it checks visual properties: scale, parts, and appearance. Then it checks physics configuration, including joints and collision geometry. Next, it tests sim-ready grasp behavior. Finally, it tests the full sequence in Isaac Sim.\\n\\nThat is a reported workflow, and it separates distinct claims. A completed sequence is still a simulation result. It does not prove physical-world reliability.\\n\\n**Visual:** Four-step ladder: 1. Visual properties — checked. 2. Physics configuration — checked. 3. Sim-ready grasp — checked. 4. Full sequence in Isaac Sim — checked. Footer: “Observed in simulation, not physical-world proof.”\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 03-c5 — NVIDIA, “5 Steps to Create SimReady Assets for Robotics with Frontier AI Models,” October 8, 2026, 00:20–00:50.\\n\\n## Scene 7 — Watch the boundary between benchmark and reality\\n\\n**Narration:** The Physical AI Podcast reports that five general-purpose models left 63 of 84 RobotWorld benchmark tasks unsolved. This is the podcast’s claim. The podcast audio has not been checked. A separately inspected RobotWorld paper confirms the benchmark count, not physical-robot performance.\\n\\nSo the uncertainty is clear: benchmark outcomes can tell you about benchmark outcomes. They cannot, by themselves, establish dependable embodied performance across physical settings.\\n\\n**Visual:** Large number: “63 of 84 tasks unsolved.” Labels: “Benchmark result” and, separated by a dashed line, “Not established: physical-world reliability.”\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 03-c6 — Physical AI Podcast, “AI Models Fail 63 of 84 Robot Tasks, Waymo Borrows $5B,” October 10, 2026, 00:22–00:34 and 02:44–02:51.\\n\\n## Scene 8 — What to do next\\n\\n**Narration:** Build around preserved native artifacts, explicit approval gates, and checks matched to the decision being automated. Keep the original input, generated file, checks run, results, and approver. Start with a clear acceptance test. Expand delegation only when the check supports the task.\\n\\nWhat should you watch next? Independent, long-term evidence from teams using connected CAD workflows on private production data. Until then, demos show what vendors can assemble, not how often these systems succeed in practice.\\n\\n**Visual:** Final checklist: “Editable artifact. Decision-specific check. Permissions. Human approver. Audit record.” Closing question: “Can we identify failure before it changes the workflow?”\\n\\n**Source on screen:** Agentic 3D synthesis, October 16, 2026. Evidence limits: 03-c1 through 03-c6.\\n\\n## Video description\\n\\nRead the companion article: [ARTICLE LINK]\\n\\nReferences:\\n\\n- Engineering.com, “Welcome to the vibe-CAD era,” September 30, 2026.\\n- MIT Computational Design and Fabrication Group, “On the Opportunities and Risks of Frontier Models for 3D Modeling, Computational Design and Robotics,” September 22, 2026.\\n- Cadalyst, “Autodesk University 2026: What the Announcements Mean for CAD Teams,” September 25, 2026.\\n- Onshape, “Introducing AI Descriptive Image Search from Onshape Labs,” October 5, 2026.\\n- NVIDIA, “5 Steps to Create SimReady Assets for Robotics with Frontier AI Models,” October 8, 2026, 00:20–00:50.\\n- Physical AI Podcast, “AI Models Fail 63 of 84 Robot Tasks, Waymo Borrows $5B,” October 10, 2026, 00:22–00:34 and 02:44–02:51.\"}"}}
