./runs/20261016T025435190699Z/logs/content-write_video_scripts-sh-a10a6ce1954440bfa3a5c143d1e72e25-attempt-1.events.jsonl:4:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"piece_id\":\"02\",\"markdown\":\"# What Agentic CAD’s Checks Actually Prove\\n\\n## Scene 1 — Opening | 0:00–0:20\\n\\n**Narration:** Which claims about agentic CAD withstand scrutiny? The answer: current evidence supports checked first drafts for bounded geometry tasks. It does not establish that generated CAD is engineering-approved, manufacturable, or ready for physical use. This is current as of October 16, 2026. This video uses AI-generated presentation.\\n\\n**Visual:** Title card. Split screen: “Checked first draft” on the left; “Engineering approval?” “Manufacturable?” “Physical use?” on the right, each marked “Not established.”\\n\\n**Source on screen:** Agentic 3D synthesis, October 16, 2026.\\n\\n## Scene 2 — What the Backflip test checked | 0:20–0:55\\n\\n**Narration:** Here is a reported fact from Engineering.com. A practitioner tested Backflip’s drawing-to-CAD output using TooTallToby drawings. For two outputs, the practitioner imported downloaded STEP files into Autodesk Fusion, applied the specified material, and checked mass. The report says both outputs were exactly correct under that test.\\n\\nThe check observed mass for two imported solids with an assigned material. That is useful. A part with the wrong mass has failed the stated criterion.\\n\\n**Visual:** A simple pipeline: drawing → Backflip STEP output → Fusion → apply material → mass check. Large label: “Observed: mass of 2 imported solids.”\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 02-c1 — Engineering.com, “Welcome to the vibe-CAD era,” September 30, 2026.\\n\\n## Scene 3 — What a mass match does not establish | 0:55–1:30\\n\\n**Narration:** This is an interpretation of that check. A mass match does not establish tolerances, design intent, manufacturing constraints, or a native editable model. It also does not show that every dimension is right.\\n\\nEngineering.com reported import defects alongside the two successful mass checks. Its Fusion add-in imported only a partial version of one model. On a seven-body wire-shelf drawing, it created one body.\\n\\nSo the evidence does not establish reliable native-CAD reconstruction or multibody performance. Passing a STEP mass check and preserving editable CAD are different claims.\\n\\n**Visual:** Comparison table. Column one: “Mass check: passed on 2 outputs.” Column two: “Native editable import: partial import observed.” Column three: “Multibody: 1 of 7 bodies observed.”\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 02-c2 — Engineering.com, “Welcome to the vibe-CAD era,” September 30, 2026.\\n\\n## Scene 4 — NVIDIA separates the checks | 1:30–2:10\\n\\n**Narration:** NVIDIA presents a different workflow in a vendor demonstration. It separates three kinds of checks: visual-reference checks, physics-property checks, and grasp-oriented simulation checks.\\n\\nFirst, it checks robot scale, parts, and appearance against references. Next, it checks configured joints, collision geometry, and physical properties. Then it tests grasps in simulation, fixes issues, and reruns the checks.\\n\\nThis is NVIDIA’s claim about its process, not independent proof of physical transfer. Each check addresses a different statement. Visual comparison checks appearance. Configured physics checks simulation properties. A simulated grasp checks success under selected simulation conditions.\\n\\n**Visual:** Three labeled gates: “Visual: scale, parts, appearance”; “Physics: joints, collision geometry, physical properties”; “Simulation: grasp test.” Final hardware icon remains outside the gates with label: “Not checked here.”\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 02-c3 — NVIDIA, “5 Steps to Create SimReady Assets for Robotics with Frontier AI Models,” October 8, 2026, 00:20–00:47.\\n\\n## Scene 5 — The operating rule | 2:10–2:45\\n\\n**Narration:** The practical rule is straightforward. Retain editable artifacts. Then match each deployment claim to the relevant check.\\n\\nUse mass or dimensions for geometry. Check feature-tree integrity for editable CAD. Check joints and collision behavior for simulation. Use physical trials for deployment.\\n\\nA valid import, a convincing image, or a simulation pass cannot substitute for the other checks. This is an interpretation drawn from the evidence, not a claim that one workflow has solved all of them.\\n\\n**Visual:** Claim-to-check map with four rows: Geometry → mass or dimensions; Editable CAD → feature-tree integrity; Simulation → joints and collision; Deployment → physical trials.\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 02-c1–02-c4 — Engineering.com, September 30, 2026; NVIDIA, October 8, 2026; MIT Computational Design and Fabrication Group, September 22, 2026.\\n\\n## Scene 6 — First draft, not final approval | 2:45–3:20\\n\\n**Narration:** The MIT Computational Design and Fabrication Group survey supports the same boundary. It says model outputs can serve as first drafts, while tolerances, structural behavior, manufacturability, and safe unsupervised operation need more evaluation.\\n\\nThat supports a first-draft role. It does not support final engineering approval. The survey also advises practitioners to keep native files editable and budget for tolerance, structural, and manufacturability checks.\\n\\n**Visual:** Document icon labeled “First draft.” Approval stamp is held back behind a checklist: tolerances, structural behavior, manufacturability, safe unsupervised operation.\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 02-c4 — MIT Computational Design and Fabrication Group, “On the Opportunities and Risks of Frontier Models for 3D Modeling, Computational Design and Robotics,” September 22, 2026.\\n\\n## Scene 7 — A robotics caution | 3:20–3:50\\n\\n**Narration:** One more source claim shows why simulation counts need careful reading. The Physical AI Podcast reports that five models ran once on each RobotWorld task, and that 63 tasks went unsolved by all of them.\\n\\nThe supplied podcast transcript has not been checked against the audio. The separately inspected RobotWorld paper supports the task counts, but this simulation result is neither independent replication nor evidence of real-robot reliability. One run per task is also sensitive to chance.\\n\\n**Visual:** “RobotWorld: 5 models, once per task; 63 unsolved tasks.” Underneath: “Simulation result ≠ real-robot reliability.” Add a small “Transcript not audio-checked” label.\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 02-c5 — Physical AI Podcast, “AI Models Fail 63 of 84 Robot Tasks, Waymo Borrows $5B,” October 10, 2026, 00:22–00:34; 02:47–02:50; 03:56–04:02.\\n\\n## Scene 8 — Close | 3:50–4:15\\n\\n**Narration:** For product and engineering leaders, treat agentic CAD as a first-draft tool with a test plan. Keep the artifact. Specify the intended use. Check the property that matters before a reviewer accepts the output.\\n\\nWatch next for repeated tests on production-like drawings, public scoring methods, disclosed settings, and independent hardware tests. Those are the checks that would narrow today’s uncertainty.\\n\\n**Visual:** Closing checklist: “Keep editable artifact. Define intended use. Run relevant check. Review before acceptance.” End card: “Next: repeated, production-like, independently checked evidence.”\\n\\n**Source on screen:** Agentic 3D synthesis, October 16, 2026.\\n\\n## Video description\\n\\nRead the companion article: [ARTICLE LINK]\\n\\nReferences:\\n\\n- Engineering.com, “Welcome to the vibe-CAD era,” September 30, 2026.\\n- NVIDIA, “5 Steps to Create SimReady Assets for Robotics with Frontier AI Models,” October 8, 2026, 00:20–00:47.\\n- MIT Computational Design and Fabrication Group, “On the Opportunities and Risks of Frontier Models for 3D Modeling, Computational Design and Robotics,” September 22, 2026.\\n- Physical AI Podcast, “AI Models Fail 63 of 84 Robot Tasks, Waymo Borrows $5B,” October 10, 2026, 00:22–00:34; 02:47–02:50; 03:56–04:02.\"}"}}
