./runs/20261016T142617879648Z/eligible.jsonl:20:{"item_id":"podcast:b836fa1b-9246-43d5-8cbb-cf1446760a6d","kind":"podcast","media_url":"https://pscrb.fm/rss/p/dts.podtrac.com/redirect.mp3/media.transistor.fm/217445d3/e1eb0b6a.mp3","published_at":"2026-10-01T09:00:00Z","publisher_id":"changelog-media","retrieved_at":"2026-10-15T20:25:21Z","segments_path":"data/assets/podcast_b836fa1b-9246-43d5-8cbb-cf1446760a6d/a0beee0f49195b6f0b08b75af793b6f6acaf56a159b58771b1c5d8f6c294b282.segments.json","source_id":"practical-ai","text_path":"data/assets/podcast_b836fa1b-9246-43d5-8cbb-cf1446760a6d/a0beee0f49195b6f0b08b75af793b6f6acaf56a159b58771b1c5d8f6c294b282.txt","title":"Open models and the future of Physical AI with NVIDIA","url":"https://pscrb.fm/rss/p/dts.podtrac.com/redirect.mp3/media.transistor.fm/217445d3/e1eb0b6a.mp3","version_id":"a0beee0f49195b6f0b08b75af793b6f6acaf56a159b58771b1c5d8f6c294b282"}
./runs/20261016T145228717481Z/logs/editorial-create_briefs-sh-draft-02-c08a6d8a9b2d40ed82e01fc918de029a-attempt-1.events.jsonl:4:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"piece_id\":\"02\",\"title\":\"The Check Is the Capability\",\"question\":\"Which claims withstand scrutiny, and where are the evidence or interpretations in tension?\",\"argument\":\"This month’s strongest CAD evidence is bounded, not broad: Backflip’s drawing-to-CAD output matched mass checks on two reference exercises after STEP import, while its photo, multibody, and feature-tree results exposed clear failure modes. Across CAD, quoting, and simulation-ready robotics, vendors are increasingly describing workflows that create or check engineering artifacts, but the available checks usually establish a narrower result than production readiness. Engineering leaders should treat geometry validity, retrieval, compatibility flags, simulation completion, and manufacturing correctness as separate acceptance gates.\",\"synthesis\":\"The evidence points to a common evaluation problem: systems can produce useful structured outputs while being checked only at one layer of the engineering stack. Engineering.com’s mass check verifies two particular reconstructed solids under a specified material assignment; Cableteque describes compatibility-based exception flags but supplies no disclosed error rate; NVIDIA describes checks for scale, joints, collision geometry, and simulated grasping. Connecting these accounts shows why a single successful CAD export or simulation run cannot establish manufacturability: each workflow needs checks that correspond to the downstream decision it is meant to support.\",\"limitations\":\"The Backflip test covers only two relatively clean reference drawings and mass agreement, not tolerances, feature-history reliability, assembly completeness, or fabricated-part conformance. Cableteque and NVIDIA describe their own products and workflows; independent test sets, reviewer decisions, error rates, and physical production or hardware outcomes would be needed to establish reliability beyond those stated scopes.\",\"claims\":[{\"claim_id\":\"02-c1\",\"statement\":\"Engineering.com reported that Backflip’s drawing-to-CAD output matched the reference mass on two TooTallToby exercises after the reporter imported downloaded STEP files into Fusion and applied 1060 aluminum.\",\"type\":\"reported_fact\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563\",\"version_id\":\"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"I can verify that Backflip got it exactly correct.\"}},{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563\",\"version_id\":\"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"Backflip aced the test.\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c2\",\"statement\":\"The same Engineering.com test found that Backflip’s Fusion add-in did not reliably recreate the tested geometry, and the system created only one body for a seven-body shelf.\",\"type\":\"reported_fact\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563\",\"version_id\":\"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"the add-in only partially recreated the geometry\"}},{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563\",\"version_id\":\"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"it only created one of the seven bodies\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c3\",\"statement\":\"Cableteque says its software builds a structured BOM from incoming drawing packages and flags likely-missing harness components using connector-and-wire compatibility, leaving flagged exceptions for estimator review.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://cableteque.com/blog/build-bom-from-wire-harness-drawing\",\"version_id\":\"1eafb0dcff5f6f5b8c341b6abe613a9a77ce26e7c0fc4eef73af6790f2ae7fe5\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"build a structured BOM\"}},{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://cableteque.com/blog/build-bom-from-wire-harness-drawing\",\"version_id\":\"1eafb0dcff5f6f5b8c341b6abe613a9a77ce26e7c0fc4eef73af6790f2ae7fe5\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"flags likely-missing terminals, seals and cavity plugs\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c4\",\"statement\":\"Cableteque’s compatibility flags establish what the company says its workflow checks, but not the rate at which its BOMs or missing-part recommendations are correct on customer drawings.\",\"type\":\"inference\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://cableteque.com/blog/build-bom-from-wire-harness-drawing\",\"version_id\":\"1eafb0dcff5f6f5b8c341b6abe613a9a77ce26e7c0fc4eef73af6790f2ae7fe5\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"based on connector and wire compatibility\"}},{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats\",\"version_id\":\"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"Only 132 cases include runnable code\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c5\",\"statement\":\"NVIDIA describes a sim-ready CAD-to-robot workflow that checks imported scale and parts, configures joints and collision geometry, then reruns simulation-ready and grasp checks after fixes.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw\",\"version_id\":\"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0004\"}},{\"item_id\":\"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw\",\"version_id\":\"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0006\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c6\",\"statement\":\"A simulation-ready asset check does not establish physical-