./runs/20261016T151820442758Z/items.jsonl:31:{"item_id":"podcast:b836fa1b-9246-43d5-8cbb-cf1446760a6d","kind":"podcast","media_url":"https://pscrb.fm/rss/p/dts.podtrac.com/redirect.mp3/media.transistor.fm/217445d3/e1eb0b6a.mp3","published_at":"2026-10-01T09:00:00Z","publisher_id":"changelog-media","retrieved_at":"2026-10-16T15:18:24Z","segments_path":"data/assets/podcast_b836fa1b-9246-43d5-8cbb-cf1446760a6d/a0beee0f49195b6f0b08b75af793b6f6acaf56a159b58771b1c5d8f6c294b282.segments.json","source_id":"practical-ai","text_path":"data/assets/podcast_b836fa1b-9246-43d5-8cbb-cf1446760a6d/a0beee0f49195b6f0b08b75af793b6f6acaf56a159b58771b1c5d8f6c294b282.txt","title":"Open models and the future of Physical AI with NVIDIA","url":"https://pscrb.fm/rss/p/dts.podtrac.com/redirect.mp3/media.transistor.fm/217445d3/e1eb0b6a.mp3","version_id":"a0beee0f49195b6f0b08b75af793b6f6acaf56a159b58771b1c5d8f6c294b282"}
./runs/20261016T153413218193Z/briefs.jsonl:1:{"piece_id":"01","title":"CAD AI Moves Into Engineering Workflows, While Verification Stays Separate","question":"What changed, and which developments matter most to this audience?","argument":"The important change is that AI is entering native CAD and simulation workflows through custom-feature generation, visual search, simulation setup, and drawing-to-CAD conversion. The evidence supports supervised use for first drafts, retrieval, and simulation preparation—not treating generated geometry as production-ready by default. For engineering leaders, the immediate decision is where these tools can accelerate bounded work while existing tolerance, manufacturability, and validation gates remain in place.","synthesis":"The evidence connects a market shift with a verification boundary. Onshape and SimScale describe AI interfaces embedded in CAD workflows, while Engineering.com independently tested a narrow drawing-to-CAD case; together, they indicate that the near-term change is more assisted entry into engineering systems, not demonstrated autonomous engineering. NVIDIA’s simulation-asset workflow adds explicit checks for visual, physics, and grasp properties, while the MIT-CDFG survey identifies tolerances and manufacturability as still untested; the analytical implication is that teams should align each release gate to the specific property actually checked.","limitations":"Engineering.com’s independent CAD test covered two successful speed-modeling drawings and one failed multibody drawing, not tolerance-critical shop drawings or production parts. Most remaining CAD and simulation evidence is vendor reporting about capabilities or workflows; representative internal test sets, disclosed failure rates, and downstream manufacturing checks are still absent.","claims":[{"claim_id":"01-c1","statement":"Engineering.com reported that Backflip passed mass checks for two drawing-to-CAD STEP outputs after import into Fusion.","type":"reported_fact","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563","version_id":"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"I can verify that Backflip got it exactly correct."}},{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563","version_id":"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"once again, Backflip aced the test."}}]},{"claim_id":"01-c2","statement":"Engineering.com reported that Backflip created only one of seven bodies for its multibody wire-shelf drawing test.","type":"reported_fact","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563","version_id":"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"it only created one of the seven bodies"}}]},{"claim_id":"01-c3","statement":"Onshape says its FeatureScript MCP Server lets AI create, test, debug, and refine custom CAD functionality from plain-language requests.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/featurescript-mcp-server-use-cases-text-code-cad","version_id":"9656a0a35f95ac0e0adb6ba1530e2759342633cbcecba8de9948699a032ab5a8","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"create, test, debug, and refine custom CAD functionality"}}]},{"claim_id":"01-c4","statement":"Onshape says AI Descriptive Image Search generates searchable descriptions from model thumbnails.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/ai-generated-thumbnail-description-search","version_id":"b2755a997c3cdda71421bbdd40ee9721d353cbc20ed2f1f80e91169757b9bbd2","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"generates searchable descriptions from model thumbnails"}}]},{"claim_id":"01-c5","statement":"Onshape says its AI Descriptive Image Search currently applies only to public documents.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/ai-generated-thumbnail-description-search","version_id":"b2755a997c3cdda71421bbdd40ee9721d353cbc20ed2f1f80e91169757b9bbd2","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"public documents only"}}]},{"claim_id":"01-c6","statement":"SimScale says its Onshape agent can propose a simulation setup, mesh and run physics, and report results in the Onshape environment.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151329","version_id":"a5a6e64fd4f2a87b5b45be5afd0c01d19fd3ee1e8b8f12be9e7e49964e03d82b","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"propose an appropriate setup, mesh and run the physics"}},{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151329","version_id":"a5a6e64fd4f2a87b5b45be5afd0c01d19fd3ee1e8b8f12be9e7e49964e03d82b","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"report the results back in the same Onshape environment"}}]},{"claim_id":"01-c7","statement":"The MIT-CDFG survey says current frontier-model outputs can serve as first drafts but do not yet show tolerance conformance or manufacturability.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats","version_id":"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"reliable enough to serve as first drafts"}},{"item_id":"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats","version_id":"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"Tolerance specifications and manufacturability have not been tested"}}]},{"claim_id":"01-c8","statement":"NVIDIA says its CAD-to-simulation workflow checks imported scale, parts, appearance, joints, collision geometry, physical properties, and a grasp test before testing a full sequence in Isaac Sim.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0004"}},{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0005"}},{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0006"}},{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0007"}}]},{"claim_id":"01-c9","statement":"The Physical AI Podcast reported that five general models left 63 of 84 robot tasks unsolved in RobotWorld.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"podcast:https://physicalaipodcast.co/episodes/010","version_id":"85cb287a2441351d8480acecc45d2ae3d6c8d1e3d180eee941a34d7bcda762d1","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0005"}}]},{"claim_id":"01-c10","statement":"Because NVIDIA’s workflow checks simulation-asset properties while the MIT-CDFG survey identifies manufacturability as untested, passing those simulation checks should not be interpreted as demonstrating manufacturability.","type":"inference","evidence":[{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0006"}},{"item_id":"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats","version_id":"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"manufacturability have not been tested"}}]}]}
./runs/20261016T153413218193Z/briefs.jsonl:2:{"piece_id":"02","title":"The Check Is the Capability","question":"Which claims withstand scrutiny, and where are the evidence or interpretations in tension?","argument":"CAD agents now merit attention as first-draft and workflow tools, but the evidence supports narrower conclusions than many product narratives imply. Engineering.com found two Backflip drawing conversions that passed a specified mass check, while the same test found a multibody failure and incomplete feature reconstruction. The practical question is not whether an agent produced or executed something, but whether the check matches the downstream engineering decision.","synthesis":"The MIT survey distinguishes editable parametric output from proof of tolerance conformance and manufacturability. Engineering.com’s Backflip test makes that distinction concrete: a material-and-mass check can verify a selected property of a converted STEP model, while a separate multibody task failed and an add-in reconstruction was incomplete. NVIDIA’s sim-ready workflow adds another bounded check set—visual reference comparison, configured physical properties, and grasp tests—so the editorial conclusion is that teams should procure and deploy agentic CAD against explicitly tested properties, not a general claim of engineering readiness.","limitations":"Backflip’s evaluation used a small, curated set of speed-modeling drawings, not production shop drawings, and its mass checks do not establish feature-level correctness, tolerance conformance, or manufacturability. Commercial workflow claims from SimScale, Onshape, and NVIDIA do not provide independent error rates, disclosed tolerance-critical test suites, or physical-transfer outcomes. The podcast’s reported robotics evaluation is supporting context only; its transcript-derived numerical details were not independently audio-verified here.","claims":[{"claim_id":"02-c1","statement":"The MIT survey says frontier models build parametric parts and assemblies, while tolerance specifications and manufacturability have not been tested on physical parts.","type":"reported_fact","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats","version_id":"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"Models build parametric parts and assemblies"}},{"item_id":"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats","version_id":"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"Tolerance specifications and manufacturability have not been tested"}}]},{"claim_id":"02-c2","statement":"Engineering.com reports importing a Backflip STEP output into Fusion, applying material, and checking its mass.","type":"reported_fact","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563","version_id":"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"import it into Fusion, apply the material, and check the mass"}}]},{"claim_id":"02-c3","statement":"Engineering.com says that Backflip got the first drawing-to-CAD mass check exactly correct.","type":"reported_fact","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563","version_id":"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"I can verify that Backflip got it exactly correct."}}]},{"claim_id":"02-c4","statement":"Engineering.com reports that Backflip created only one of seven bodies on a multibody drawing-to-CAD task.","type":"reported_fact","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563","version_id":"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"it only created one of the seven bodies"}}]},{"claim_id":"02-c5","statement":"Engineering.com reports that Backflip’s Fusion add-in only partially recreated geometry for one tested part.","type":"reported_fact","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563","version_id":"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"the add-in only partially recreated the geometry"}}]},{"claim_id":"02-c6","statement":"A selected-output mass check supports mass agreement for that test, not tolerance conformance or manufacturability.","type":"inference","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563","version_id":"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"apply the material and check the mass"}},{"item_id":"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats","version_id":"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"confirming conformance to tolerances and manufacturability requires further evaluation"}}]},{"claim_id":"02-c7","statement":"SimScale says its Engineering AI Agent can propose a setup, mesh a CAD model, run physics, and report results in Onshape.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151329","version_id":"a5a6e64fd4f2a87b5b45be5afd0c01d19fd3ee1e8b8f12be9e7e49964e03d82b","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"propose an appropriate setup, mesh and run the physics"}}]},{"claim_id":"02-c8","statement":"Onshape says its FeatureScript MCP Server lets LLMs help create, test, debug, and refine custom CAD functionality.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/featurescript-mcp-server-use-cases-text-code-cad","version_id":"9656a0a35f95ac0e0adb6ba1530e2759342633cbcecba8de9948699a032ab5a8","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"create, test, debug, and refine custom CAD functionality"}}]},{"claim_id":"02-c9","statement":"NVIDIA says its CAD-to-simulation workflow checks imported robot scale, parts, and appearance against references.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0004"}}]},{"claim_id":"02-c10","statement":"NVIDIA says its workflow configures joints, collision geometry, and physical properties for arms and grippers.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0005"}}]},{"claim_id":"02-c11","statement":"NVIDIA says its workflow runs sim-ready foundation checks and a grasp test, then fixes issues and reruns them.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0006"}}]},{"claim_id":"02-c12","statement":"The Physical AI Podcast reports that the operator in a Black Forest Labs-commissioned evaluation was blind to which model was driving.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"podcast:https://physicalaipodcast.co/episodes/008","version_id":"1cb4eefeb597341d29e650ad7c122c9eea10b1aa146aef9c4a6214acdf2c2983","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0044"}}]}]}
./runs/20261016T153413218193Z/briefs.jsonl:3:{"piece_id":"03","title":"Adopt the Workflow, Not the Unchecked Output","question":"What do these developments imply for the audience, and what should they watch next?","argument":"The immediate opportunity is to use AI for first-draft CAD, reusable feature development, model search, and simulation preparation while keeping release decisions behind checks matched to engineering risk. Teams should watch for evidence that generated geometry passes tolerance, manufacturability, and downstream validation—not merely that it forms a valid model or completes a simulation workflow. The most useful next signal will be disclosed, representative evaluations that separate model output from the CAD, simulation, and review systems around it.","synthesis":"The evidence distinguishes useful workflow automation from verified engineering outcome. MIT’s survey finds editable CAD outputs promising but identifies missing physical-part tolerance and manufacturability tests; Engineering.com’s Backflip trial shows a narrow mass check can validate a specific output while another drawing fails a basic multibody requirement. Meanwhile, Onshape and NVIDIA describe product pathways for text-to-CAD features and CAD-to-simulation preparation, making the operational question for engineering leaders which independent release checks remain mandatory after AI has accelerated upstream work.","limitations":"Evidence for production readiness remains incomplete. Engineering.com tested a small, curated set of Backflip drawings, while the Onshape and NVIDIA descriptions are vendor claims; no accepted evidence provides an independently replicated comparison of AI-generated CAD features or simulation-ready assets against production tolerances, manufacturability, or real-world transfer. The robotics evidence is supporting context, not evidence that CAD workflows transfer physically.","claims":[{"claim_id":"03-c1","statement":"The MIT survey says its record does not show that frontier-model outputs meet engineering requirements such as tolerances or manufacturability.","type":"reported_fact","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats","version_id":"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"does not yet show that they meet engineering requirements"}}]},{"claim_id":"03-c2","statement":"Engineering.com reported that Backflip created only one body from a seven-body drawing-to-CAD test.","type":"reported_fact","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563","version_id":"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"it only created one of the seven bodies"}}]},{"claim_id":"03-c3","statement":"Engineering.com reported that its Backflip drawing-to-CAD test imported a STEP file into Fusion, applied material, and checked mass.","type":"reported_fact","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563","version_id":"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"import it into Fusion, apply the material, and check the mass"}}]},{"claim_id":"03-c4","statement":"Onshape says its FeatureScript MCP Server enables engineers to have AI help create, test, debug, and refine custom CAD functionality from plain-language requests.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/featurescript-mcp-server-use-cases-text-code-cad","version_id":"9656a0a35f95ac0e0adb6ba1530e2759342633cbcecba8de9948699a032ab5a8","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"create, test, debug, and refine custom CAD functionality"}}]},{"claim_id":"03-c5","statement":"NVIDIA describes checking an imported robot’s scale, parts, and appearance against references in its CAD-to-simulation workflow.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0004"}}]},{"claim_id":"03-c6","statement":"NVIDIA describes using grasp tests and selected simulation-readiness checks before running a full sequence in Isaac Sim.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0006"}},{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0007"}}]},{"claim_id":"03-c7","statement":"The Physical AI Podcast reports that five general models left 63 of 84 robot tasks unsolved in RobotWorld, whose program checked whether each job was completed.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"podcast:https://physicalaipodcast.co/episodes/010","version_id":"85cb287a2441351d8480acecc45d2ae3d6c8d1e3d180eee941a34d7bcda762d1","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0005"}},{"item_id":"podcast:https://physicalaipodcast.co/episodes/010","version_id":"85cb287a2441351d8480acecc45d2ae3d6c8d1e3d180eee941a34d7bcda762d1","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0036"}}]},{"claim_id":"03-c8","statement":"Because the survey identifies untested physical-part tolerances and manufacturability while NVIDIA describes simulation-readiness checks, simulation-ready status should not be treated as release-ready manufacturing evidence.","type":"inference","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats","version_id":"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"Tolerance specifications and manufacturability have not been tested on physical parts."}},{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0006"}}]}]}
./runs/20261016T153413218193Z/logs/content-write_video_scripts-sh-018be8776718434fabd9032d5731ae4f-attempt-1.events.jsonl:4:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"piece_id\":\"03\",\"markdown\":\"# Adopt the Workflow, Not the Unchecked Output\\n\\n## Scene 1 — The answer\\n\\n**Narration:** What should engineering leaders do with AI-generated CAD and simulation assets? Adopt the workflow, not the unchecked output. Use AI for first drafts, reusable feature development, model search, and simulation preparation. But keep release decisions behind checks that match the engineering risk. This is a reported-evidence review, as of October 16, 2026. Disclosure: this video uses AI-generated presentation.\\n\\n**Visual:** Title on screen. A pipeline: “AI draft” → “Engineering checks” → “Release.” The release gate is highlighted.\\n\\n**Source on screen:** MIT Computational Design and Fabrication Group, “On the Opportunities and Risks of Frontier Models for 3D Modeling, Computational Design and Robotics,” September 22, 2026.\\n\\n## Scene 2 — The core test\\n\\n**Narration:** The core test is simple. A capability claim holds only as far as the check that observed it. A valid solid does not prove a part is manufacturable. A good-looking model does not prove its dimensions are right. And a completed simulation workflow does not prove physical transfer.\\n\\n**Visual:** Three paired cards: “Valid model ≠ manufacturable part,” “Mass check ≠ full drawing conformance,” and “Simulation-ready ≠ manufacturing-release ready.”\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 03-c1 — MIT Computational Design and Fabrication Group, “On the Opportunities and Risks of Frontier Models for 3D Modeling, Computational Design and Robotics,” September 22, 2026.\\n\\n## Scene 3 — What the survey does and does not show\\n\\n**Narration:** MIT’s survey reports that its record does not show frontier-model outputs meeting engineering requirements such as tolerances or manufacturability. That is a reported finding about the public evidence record. It does not prove that no model can meet those requirements.\\n\\nThe survey identifies the missing check: tolerance specifications and manufacturability have not been tested on physical parts in the CAD evidence it reviewed.\\n\\n**Visual:** A checklist labeled “Observed in the record”: editable CAD. A second checklist labeled “Not tested on physical parts”: tolerances, manufacturability. Add “Claim boundary: first draft, not production proof.”\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 03-c1 — MIT Computational Design and Fabrication Group, “On the Opportunities and Risks of Frontier Models for 3D Modeling, Computational Design and Robotics,” September 22, 2026.\\n\\n## Scene 4 — A narrow check can still be useful\\n\\n**Narration:** Engineering.com reported a concrete Backflip drawing-to-CAD test. The reporter imported a STEP file into Fusion, applied material, and checked mass. That check observed mass after import under the selected material assignment.\\n\\nBut the same report found a different failure: from a seven-body drawing, Backflip created only one body. The lesson is not that one result cancels the other. It is that each check answers a different question.\\n\\n**Visual:** Split screen. Left: “Mass check” with STEP → Fusion → material → mass. Right: “Assembly requirement” with seven body icons, only one highlighted. Caption: “What was checked versus what was claimed.”\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 03-c3, 03-c2 — Engineering.com, “Welcome to the vibe-CAD era,” September 30, 2026.\\n\\n## Scene 5 — Workflow claims versus release evidence\\n\\n**Narration:** Onshape says its FeatureScript MCP Server lets engineers use plain-language requests to have AI help create, test, debug, and refine custom CAD functionality. This is Onshape’s product claim, not an independent outcome evaluation.\\n\\nThe workflow may be useful for reusable internal features. But “test” in that description does not establish a company’s release criteria. Keep code review, geometry checks, and domain-specific validation in the process.\\n\\n**Visual:** Plain-language request → FeatureScript → custom feature. A separate release-gate panel lists: code review, geometry checks, domain validation.\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 03-c4 — Onshape, “5 Ways Teams Can Leverage the Onshape FeatureScript MCP Server,” September 30, 2026.\\n\\n## Scene 6 — Simulation checks have a boundary\\n\\n**Narration:** NVIDIA describes a CAD-to-simulation workflow that checks an imported robot’s scale, parts, and appearance against references. NVIDIA also describes grasp tests and selected simulation-readiness checks before a full sequence runs in Isaac Sim. These are NVIDIA’s workflow claims.\\n\\nThose checks support claims about the simulation asset and the selected sequence. My interpretation is that simulation-ready status should not be treated as manufacturing-release evidence. MIT identifies untested physical-part tolerance and manufacturability requirements. NVIDIA describes simulation-readiness checks. Those are different gates.\\n\\n**Visual:** Two labeled gates. “Simulation-ready”: scale, parts, appearance, grasp test, selected checks. “Manufacturing release”: tolerances, manufacturability, physical-part validation. A clear “not equivalent” symbol between them.\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 03-c5, 03-c6, 03-c8 — NVIDIA, “5 Steps to Create SimReady Assets for Robotics with Frontier AI Models,” October 8, 2026, 0:20–0:28 and 0:37–0:50; MIT Computational Design and Fabrication Group, September 22, 2026.\\n\\n## Scene 7 — Supporting robotics context\\n\\n**Narration:** The Physical AI Podcast reports that five general models left 63 of 84 RobotWorld tasks unsolved. It says a program checked whether each job was completed. This is a source claim about task completion in that program. It is not evidence about CAD quality, tolerances, or production transfer. It does reinforce the need for outcome checks when systems act through tools.\\n\\n**Visual:** On-screen number: “63 of 84 tasks unsolved.” Below it: “Observed check: job completed?” A footnote: “Supporting robotics context; not CAD evidence.”\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 03-c7 — Physical AI Podcast, “AI Models Fail 63 of 84 Robot Tasks, Waymo Borrows $5B,” October 10, 2026, 0:28–0:34 and 2:23–2:27.\\n\\n## Scene 8 — What to do next\\n\\n**Narration:** Put AI where iteration is cheap and review is strong: first drafts, reusable feature development, search, conversion, and simulation setup. Then define explicit release gates. Check body count and topology for assemblies. Check dimensions and tolerances for parts. Check process-specific manufacturability for production. Use representative physical tests where hardware behavior matters.\\n\\nWatch next for disclosed, representative evaluations. They should separate model output from the CAD, simulation, and review systems around it. They should state what was checked, on what inputs, and where the system failed.\\n\\n**Visual:** Closing checklist: “Use workflow automation” and “Match every release claim to its check.” End card: “Watch for disclosed evaluations with representative inputs and failure reporting.”\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 03-c1, 03-c2, 03-c3, 03-c8 — MIT Computational Design and Fabrication Group, September 22, 2026; Engineering.com, September 30, 2026.\\n\\n## Video description\\n\\nCompanion article: [ARTICLE LINK]\\n\\nReferences:\\n\\n- MIT Computational Design and Fabrication Group, “On the Opportunities and Risks of Frontier Models for 3D Modeling, Computational Design and Robotics,” September 22, 2026. https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/\\n- Engineering.com, “Welcome to the vibe-CAD era,” September 30, 2026. https://www.engineering.com/welcome-to-the-vibe-cad-era/\\n- Onshape, “5 Ways Teams Can Leverage the Onshape FeatureScript MCP Server,” September 30, 2026. https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/featurescript-mcp-server-use-cases-text-code-cad\\n- NVIDIA, “5 Steps to Create SimReady Assets for Robotics with Frontier AI Models,” October 8, 2026, 0:20–0:28 and 0:37–0:50. https://www.youtube.com/shorts/H5UaOdIZERw\\n- Physical AI Podcast, “AI Models Fail 63 of 84 Robot Tasks, Waymo Borrows $5B,” October 10, 2026, 0:28–0:34 and 2:23–2:27. https://dts.podtrac.com/redirect.mp3/op3.dev/e,pg=83ccf97e-1d88-565b-ad28-0411c3a101af/physicalaipodcast.co/audio/episode-010.mp3\"}"}}
