./runs/20261016T164638172713Z/decisions.jsonl:1:{"item_id":"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats","version_id":"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5","decision":"accept","reason":"This September 22 survey is fresh and directly relevant to editable 3D scenes, parametric CAD, manufacturability, and robotics as supporting context. It provides a citable passage and a useful evidence boundary: the authors say they reviewed 1,646 public posts across 707 cases, but only 132 cases have runnable code, so its reported benchmark and capability figures mostly synthesize demonstrations and vendor reports rather than independent replication. Its strongest contribution is the scrutiny framework—CAD outputs may be editable and benchmark-valid without proving tolerance conformance or manufacturability, and robotics simulation results do not establish robust physical transfer. It is a research-oriented synthesis rather than an independent confirmation of every underlying claim, so repeated underlying announcements should not be counted as corroboration.","piece_ids":["01","02","03"]}
./runs/20261016T164638172713Z/decisions.jsonl:12:{"item_id":"podcast:b836fa1b-9246-43d5-8cbb-cf1446760a6d","version_id":"a0beee0f49195b6f0b08b75af793b6f6acaf56a159b58771b1c5d8f6c294b282","decision":"accept","reason":"This is fresh, timestamped robotics context from NVIDIA's VP of Cosmos Lab, relevant to intelligent systems acting in physical space and simulation-ready world representations. At 00:23:59–00:24:19, the speaker describes world simulation as taking a current observation and action sequence and outputting a predicted future, a narrow attributable claim about NVIDIA's framing rather than evidence of real-world transfer. It supports scrutiny of what a simulator checks versus what physical deployment would require; the sampled transcript supplies no disclosed benchmark, hardware trial, or independent verification of performance.","piece_ids":["02","03"]}
