./runs/20261016T042241540057Z/items.jsonl:12:{"item_id":"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats","kind":"feed","media_url":null,"published_at":"2026-09-22T00:00:00Z","publisher_id":"mit-cdfg","retrieved_at":"2026-10-16T04:22:43Z","segments_path":null,"source_id":"mit-cdfg-survey","text_path":"data/assets/feed_https_mit-cdfg.github.io_Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics_view-stats/08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5.txt","title":"On the Opportunities and Risks of Frontier Models for 3D Modeling, Computational Design and Robotics","url":"https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/","version_id":"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5"}
./runs/20261016T042241540057Z/items.jsonl:20:{"item_id":"podcast:b836fa1b-9246-43d5-8cbb-cf1446760a6d","kind":"podcast","media_url":"https://pscrb.fm/rss/p/dts.podtrac.com/redirect.mp3/media.transistor.fm/217445d3/e1eb0b6a.mp3","published_at":"2026-10-01T09:00:00Z","publisher_id":"changelog-media","retrieved_at":"2026-10-16T04:22:48Z","segments_path":"data/assets/podcast_b836fa1b-9246-43d5-8cbb-cf1446760a6d/a0beee0f49195b6f0b08b75af793b6f6acaf56a159b58771b1c5d8f6c294b282.segments.json","source_id":"practical-ai","text_path":"data/assets/podcast_b836fa1b-9246-43d5-8cbb-cf1446760a6d/a0beee0f49195b6f0b08b75af793b6f6acaf56a159b58771b1c5d8f6c294b282.txt","title":"Open models and the future of Physical AI with NVIDIA","url":"https://pscrb.fm/rss/p/dts.podtrac.com/redirect.mp3/media.transistor.fm/217445d3/e1eb0b6a.mp3","version_id":"a0beee0f49195b6f0b08b75af793b6f6acaf56a159b58771b1c5d8f6c294b282"}
./runs/20261016T153413218193Z/logs/content-write_video_scripts-sh-39a6e998567649dbafb8b56e97f9b050-attempt-1.events.jsonl:4:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"piece_id\":\"01\",\"markdown\":\"# CAD AI Moves Into Engineering Workflows, While Verification Stays Separate\\n\\n## Scene 1 — The answer\\n\\n**Narration:** What changed, and which developments matter most to engineering leaders? AI is moving into native CAD and simulation workflows. But the evidence supports it as an assistant for bounded engineering work, not as a substitute for production validation. As of October 16, 2026, the immediate decision is where these tools can accelerate reviewable work while tolerance, manufacturability, and normal validation gates remain in place. Disclosure: this video uses AI-generated presentation.\\n\\n**Visual:** Title card. Split screen: “AI-assisted workflow entry” on the left; “Production validation” on the right. A bold divider reads: “A successful check proves only what it checked.”\\n\\n**Source on screen:** Agentic 3D, “CAD AI Moves Into Engineering Workflows, While Verification Stays Separate,” October 16, 2026.\\n\\n## Scene 2 — A narrow independent check\\n\\n**Narration:** The clearest narrow test comes from Engineering.com. This is a reported fact. The publication imported two Backflip drawing-to-CAD STEP outputs into Fusion, applied material, and checked mass. It reported that both outputs matched the target mass. That check supports a claim about mass for those two models, under the stated material assumption. It does not check tolerances, feature intent, or manufacturability.\\n\\n**Visual:** Diagram: drawing → Backflip STEP output → Fusion import → apply 1060 aluminum → mass check. Two green labels: “Target mass matched.” A gray boundary box: “Not checked: tolerances, feature intent, manufacturability.”\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 01-c1 — Engineering.com, “Welcome to the vibe-CAD era,” September 30, 2026. https://www.engineering.com/welcome-to-the-vibe-cad-era/\\n\\n## Scene 3 — The same workflow fails another check\\n\\n**Narration:** The same report establishes the boundary. Engineering.com reported that Backflip created only one of seven bodies for a multibody wire-shelf drawing. Here, the check was simple: count generated bodies against the drawing. It failed. This matters because two successful mass checks do not establish complete assembly structure on another input.\\n\\n**Visual:** A seven-body shelf drawing beside a generated model. Counter animates: “Expected: 7 bodies. Created: 1 body.” Core-test label: “Observed check: body count.”\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 01-c2 — Engineering.com, “Welcome to the vibe-CAD era,” September 30, 2026. https://www.engineering.com/welcome-to-the-vibe-cad-era/\\n\\n## Scene 4 — CAD workflow entry points\\n\\n**Narration:** Several releases target workflow entry points rather than final signoff. Onshape says its FeatureScript MCP Server lets AI create, test, debug, and refine custom CAD functionality from plain-language requests. That is Onshape’s product claim. The observed basis here is its own product description, not an independent test of whether generated features preserve a company’s engineering standards in every case.\\n\\nOnshape also says its AI Descriptive Image Search generates searchable descriptions from model thumbnails. It says the feature currently applies only to public documents. That is a retrieval claim. The relevant check is image-to-text search over thumbnails. It does not establish geometry equivalence, metadata correctness, or use on a private production library.\\n\\n**Visual:** Two panels. Left: prompt → FeatureScript → “create / test / debug / refine.” Right: thumbnail → generated description → search results. Under both: “What was checked?” Left: “Vendor-described workflow.” Right: “Thumbnail-based retrieval.”\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 01-c3 — Onshape, “5 Ways Teams Can Leverage the Onshape FeatureScript MCP Server,” September 30, 2026. https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/featurescript-mcp-server-use-cases-text-code-cad/\\n\\n01-c4, 01-c5 — Onshape, “Introducing AI Descriptive Image Search from Onshape Labs,” October 5, 2026. https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/ai-generated-thumbnail-description-search\\n\\n## Scene 5 — Simulation preparation is not validation\\n\\n**Narration:** Simulation is following the same pattern. SimScale says its Onshape agent can propose a simulation setup, mesh and run physics, and report results in the Onshape environment. This is a reported vendor claim about workflow capability. It does not show representative accuracy, appropriate boundary conditions, or reliability across models.\\n\\nNVIDIA makes a more specific verification boundary explicit. NVIDIA says its CAD-to-simulation workflow checks imported scale, parts, and appearance against references; then joints, collision geometry, and physical properties; then simulation-ready checks and a grasp test before a full sequence in Isaac Sim.\\n\\n**Visual:** A staged checklist: visual properties; physics properties; simulation-ready checks plus grasp test; full Isaac Sim sequence. Each stage has a label: “Checked property.” A separate red-outlined box reads: “Manufacturability: not checked here.”\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 01-c6 — Engineering.com, “Multiphysics gets MCP server and Onshape gets SimScale agent,” September 23, 2026. https://www.engineering.com/multiphysics-gets-mcp-server-and-onshape-gets-simscale-agent/\\n\\n01-c8 — NVIDIA, “5 Steps to Create SimReady Assets for Robotics with Frontier AI Models,” October 8, 2026, 00:20–00:50. https://www.youtube.com/shorts/H5UaOdIZERw\\n\\n## Scene 6 — The release-gate rule\\n\\n**Narration:** The MIT-CDFG survey says current frontier-model outputs can serve as first drafts, but do not yet show tolerance conformance or manufacturability. That is the survey’s claim based on its reviewed record, not a universal result for every CAD workflow.\\n\\nMy interpretation follows from the checks just shown: passing NVIDIA’s simulation-asset workflow should not be interpreted as demonstrating manufacturability. Match each release gate to the property it actually observes.\\n\\n**Visual:** Gate map: “Mass” → mass check. “Assembly completeness” → body count. “Simulation behavior” → configured properties, grasp test, full sequence. “Manufacturability” → separate tolerance and design-for-manufacturing gate, marked “evidence absent.”\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 01-c7 — MIT-CDFG, “On the Opportunities and Risks of Frontier Models for 3D Modeling, Computational Design and Robotics,” September 22, 2026. https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/\\n\\n01-c10 — Interpretation from NVIDIA’s simulation checks and MIT-CDFG’s stated manufacturability gap.\\n\\n## Scene 7 — Supporting robotics context and next step\\n\\n**Narration:** Supporting context from robotics points in the same direction. The Physical AI Podcast reported that five general models left 63 of 84 RobotWorld tasks unsolved. This is the podcast’s report, and it checks those tasks only. It does not measure CAD generation or validate an engineering product.\\n\\nFor engineering leaders, deploy these tools first where outputs remain reviewable and failure is cheap: reusable feature drafts, retrieval, drawing-to-CAD intake, and simulation preparation. Keep tolerance review, design-for-manufacturing checks, and normal validation gates separate. Watch next for disclosed test sets that connect generated CAD to dimensions, tolerances, manufacturability, and downstream physical results.\\n\\n**Visual:** Four approved starting lanes: feature drafts, retrieval, drawing-to-CAD intake, simulation preparation. Below them, a protected production gate: tolerance review → DFM checks → normal validation. End card: “Watch for property-specific, disclosed tests.”\\n\\n**Source on screen:** 01-c9 — Physical AI Podcast, “AI Models Fail 63 of 84 Robot Tasks, Waymo Borrows $5B,” October 10, 2026, 00:28–00:34. https://dts.podtrac.com/redirect.mp3/op3.dev/e,pg=83ccf97e-1d88-565b-ad28-0411c3a101af/physicalaipodcast.co/audio/episode-010.mp3\\n\\n## Video description\\n\\nArticle: [ARTICLE LINK]\\n\\nReferences:\\n\\n- Engineering.com, “Welcome to the vibe-CAD era,” September 30, 2026. https://www.engineering.com/welcome-to-the-vibe-cad-era/\\n- Onshape, “5 Ways Teams Can Leverage the Onshape FeatureScript MCP Server,” September 30, 2026. https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/featurescript-mcp-server-use-cases-text-code-cad/\\n- Onshape, “Introducing AI Descriptive Image Search from Onshape Labs,” October 5, 2026. https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/ai-generated-thumbnail-description-search\\n- Engineering.com, “Multiphysics gets MCP server and Onshape gets SimScale agent,” September 23, 2026. https://www.engineering.com/multiphysics-gets-mcp-server-and-onshape-gets-simscale-agent/\\n- MIT-CDFG, “On the Opportunities and Risks of Frontier Models for 3D Modeling, Computational Design and Robotics,” September 22, 2026. https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/\\n- NVIDIA, “5 Steps to Create SimReady Assets for Robotics with Frontier AI Models,” October 8, 2026, 00:20–00:50. https://www.youtube.com/shorts/H5UaOdIZERw\\n- Physical AI Podcast, “AI Models Fail 63 of 84 Robot Tasks, Waymo Borrows $5B,” October 10, 2026, 00:28–00:34. https://dts.podtrac.com/redirect.mp3/op3.dev/e,pg=83ccf97e-1d88-565b-ad28-0411c3a101af/physicalaipodcast.co/audio/episode-010.mp3\"}"}}
