./runs/20261016T140335482061Z/candidates/podcasts.jsonl:3:{"item_id":"podcast:b836fa1b-9246-43d5-8cbb-cf1446760a6d","source_id":"practical-ai","publisher_id":"changelog-media","kind":"podcast","title":"Open models and the future of Physical AI with NVIDIA","url":"https://pscrb.fm/rss/p/dts.podtrac.com/redirect.mp3/media.transistor.fm/217445d3/e1eb0b6a.mp3","published_at":"2026-10-01T09:00:00Z","retrieved_at":"2026-10-16T14:03:40Z","media_url":"https://pscrb.fm/rss/p/dts.podtrac.com/redirect.mp3/media.transistor.fm/217445d3/e1eb0b6a.mp3"}
./runs/20261016T153413218193Z/logs/editorial-create_briefs-sh-draft-03-43f96b80c4f64e94a707e3c8e26201af-attempt-1.events.jsonl:4:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"piece_id\":\"03\",\"title\":\"What to Watch After AI Enters the CAD Workflow\",\"question\":\"What do these developments imply for the audience, and what should they watch next?\",\"argument\":\"The near-term opportunity is not autonomous release-ready design, but faster creation, retrieval, and simulation preparation inside engineering workflows that retain explicit verification gates. Teams should watch whether vendors publish task-specific evidence that generated geometry, parametric features, and simulation-ready assets survive the checks that matter downstream: tolerances, manufacturability, boundary conditions, and physical transfer. The strongest adoption signal will be reproducible results on the team's own representative parts and processes, not broader agentic claims.\",\"synthesis\":\"The evidence points to a practical division of labor: frontier models can accelerate drafts and interface work, while engineering teams must preserve native artifacts and independently test the properties the generation workflow did not check. MIT’s review distinguishes editable CAD output from tolerance and manufacturability validation; Engineering.com’s Backflip test shows that a mass check can verify a narrow property of two outputs while leaving feature reconstruction failures and shop-drawing generalization unresolved. Onshape and NVIDIA show vendors moving AI into parametric-feature authoring and CAD-to-simulation preparation, so the next decision is less whether to experiment than which release gates, logs, and representative test suites must remain outside the model’s authority.\",\"limitations\":\"The available evidence is uneven: Backflip received a small curated editorial test, while the Onshape and NVIDIA workflow claims are vendor descriptions. No accepted source supplies a comparative, independently replicated evaluation of AI-generated features or simulation-ready assets against production tolerances, manufacturability, or physical transfer; teams should treat local pilots as necessary evidence rather than assume these workflows generalize.\",\"claims\":[{\"claim_id\":\"03-c1\",\"statement\":\"The MIT survey reports that frontier models are useful for first-draft 3D and CAD work, but its record does not establish conformance to tolerances or manufacturability.\",\"type\":\"reported_fact\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats\",\"version_id\":\"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"confirming conformance to tolerances and manufacturability requires further evaluation\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"03-c2\",\"statement\":\"Engineering.com independently found that two Backflip drawing-to-CAD STEP outputs matched the reference mass after import into Fusion and application of 1060 aluminum.\",\"type\":\"reported_fact\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563\",\"version_id\":\"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"I can verify that Backflip got it exactly correct.\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"03-c3\",\"statement\":\"The same Engineering.com test found Backflip created only one body for a seven-body drawing, showing that a successful narrow mass check does not establish broad drawing-to-CAD reliability.\",\"type\":\"reported_fact\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563\",\"version_id\":\"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"it only created one of the seven bodies\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"03-c4\",\"statement\":\"Onshape says its FeatureScript MCP Server lets engineers use LLMs to create, test, debug, and refine custom CAD functionality from plain-language requests.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/featurescript-mcp-server-use-cases-text-code-cad\",\"version_id\":\"9656a0a35f95ac0e0adb6ba1530e2759342633cbcecba8de9948699a032ab5a8\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"create, test, debug, and refine custom CAD functionality\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"03-c5\",\"statement\":\"NVIDIA describes a CAD-to-simulation workflow that checks scale, parts, appearance, joints, collision geometry, physical properties, and a grasp test before a full Isaac Sim sequence.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw\",\"version_id\":\"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0004\"}},{\"item_id\":\"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw\",\"version_id\":\"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0005\"}},{\"item_id\":\"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw\",\"version_id\":\"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0006\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"03-c6\",\"statement\":\"Because the MIT survey identifies untested tolerances and manufacturability while NVIDIA’s described checks concern simulation readiness, teams should add independent production-oriented checks before promoting generated geometry to release status.\",\"type\":\"inference\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats\",\"version_id\":\"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"Tolerance specifications and manufacturability have not been tested on physical parts.\"}},{\"item_id\":\"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw\",\"version_id\":\"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0006\"}}]}]}"}}
./runs/20261016T153413218193Z/logs/editorial-create_briefs-sh-draft-02-76d200eb182b4db989a5bb0d29b85b0e-attempt-1.events.jsonl:4:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"piece_id\":\"02\",\"title\":\"The Check Is the Capability\",\"question\":\"Which claims withstand scrutiny, and where are the evidence or interpretations in tension?\",\"argument\":\"This month’s CAD agents have crossed an important threshold: they can produce editable geometry and execute simulation workflows that merit engineering attention. But the available checks are narrower than many product narratives—Backflip’s drawing-to-CAD outputs passed two mass checks, while photo-derived geometry and feature reconstruction exposed failures. The practical dividing line is whether a workflow verifies the property that matters: a valid or mass-matched model is not yet evidence of tolerance conformance, manufacturability, or correct simulation assumptions.\",\"synthesis\":\"The MIT survey separates editable first-draft CAD from physical-part validation, while Engineering.com’s Backflip test shows why that distinction matters in practice: two curated drawing conversions matched reference mass, yet another multi-body conversion failed and the Fusion add-in omitted geometry. NVIDIA’s CAD-to-simulation workflow adds a related boundary: checking scale, joints, collision geometry, and a grasp can establish simulation readiness for those checks, not real-world transfer. Together, these sources suggest a procurement and deployment rule: evaluate agentic CAD by the downstream decision its explicit test actually covers, rather than treating successful generation or execution as a general engineering guarantee.\",\"limitations\":\"The Backflip evaluation covers a small, curated set of speed-modeling drawings rather than production shop drawings, and its mass checks do not inspect every feature or manufacturing constraint. Onshape, SimScale, and NVIDIA describe useful commercial workflows, but their supplied evidence does not disclose independent error rates, test suites, or outcomes on tolerance-critical and production conditions.\",\"claims\":[{\"claim_id\":\"02-c1\",\"statement\":\"The MIT survey reports that frontier CAD systems can produce editable parametric parts and assemblies, but says tolerance specifications and manufacturability have not been tested on physical parts.\",\"type\":\"reported_fact\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats\",\"version_id\":\"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"Tolerance specifications and manufacturability have not been tested\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c2\",\"statement\":\"Engineering.com independently reports that two Backflip drawing-to-CAD STEP outputs matched the TooTallToby reference mass after import into Fusion and application of 1060 aluminum.\",\"type\":\"reported_fact\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563\",\"version_id\":\"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"I can verify that Backflip got it exactly correct.\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c3\",\"statement\":\"The same Engineering.com test found that Backflip produced only one of seven bodies on a multibody drawing-to-CAD task.\",\"type\":\"reported_fact\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563\",\"version_id\":\"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"it only created one of the seven bodies\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c4\",\"statement\":\"A mass match on selected drawing-to-CAD outputs is stronger evidence than a visual demo, but it still does not establish tolerance conformance or manufacturability.\",\"type\":\"inference\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563\",\"version_id\":\"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"checking the mass of the part\"}},{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats\",\"version_id\":\"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"confirming conformance to tolerances and manufacturability requires further evaluation\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c5\",\"statement\":\"SimScale says its Onshape agent can propose a simulation setup, mesh and run physics from a CAD model, then report results in Onshape.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151329\",\"version_id\":\"a5a6e64fd4f2a87b5b45be5afd0c01d19fd3ee1e8b8f12be9e7e49964e03d82b\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"propose an appropriate setup, mesh and run the physics\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c6\",\"statement\":\"NVIDIA says its CAD-to-simulation workflow checks imported scale, parts, appearance, joints, collision geometry, physical properties, and a grasp test before a full Isaac Sim sequence.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw\",\"version_id\":\"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0004\"}},{\"item_id\":\"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw\",\"version_id\":\"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0006\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c7\",\"statement\":\"The Physical AI Podcast reports that Black Forest Labs commissioned a blind third-party evaluation with 10 manipulation tasks, three attempts per task, a shared time window, and a shared scoring sheet.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"podcast:https://physicalaipodcast.co/episodes/008\",\"version_id\":\"1cb4eefeb597341d29e650ad7c122c9eea10b1aa146aef9c4a6214acdf2c2983\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0036\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c8\",\"statement\":\"The disclosed third-party robotics protocol is a more interpretable performance check than an unqualified simulation ranking, but its 30 total attempts remain too small to establish broad reliability.\",\"type\":\"inference\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"podcast:https://physicalaipodcast.co/episodes/008\",\"version_id\":\"1cb4eefeb597341d29e650ad7c122c9eea10b1aa146aef9c4a6214acdf2c2983\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0041\"}},{\"item_id\":\"podcast:https://physicalaipodcast.co/episodes/008\",\"version_id\":\"1cb4eefeb597341d29e650ad7c122c9eea10b1aa146aef9c4a6214acdf2c2983\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0044\"}}]}]}"}}
