./runs/20261015T202519414242Z/logs/editorial-select_items-sh-511b7a0e23cf42008945c8e8ac14dfcc-attempt-1.events.jsonl:4:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"item_id\":\"feed:/resources/materials/physical-vapor-deposition/\",\"version_id\":\"fcf9079ad45657e09903d78c29d7b8ae301b4348d9c2d777777b5d1e831378a0\",\"decision\":\"reject\",\"reason\":\"Although fresh and timestamped, this is a general manufacturing-process explainer rather than a development in frontier AI for 3D modeling, CAD, or physical AI. It supplies no model capability, evaluation, simulation-ready asset workflow, or checkable AI-native product evidence, so it fails relevance and novelty criteria.\",\"piece_ids\":[]}\n{\"item_id\":\"feed:/resources/materials/recrystallization-of-metals/\",\"version_id\":\"ded55799bfd57b9e786dbfccb038115b95d469ba2c6a470457c0869c6e17bd6c\",\"decision\":\"reject\",\"reason\":\"This recent, timestamped metallurgy primer is credible only as general background and contains no frontier-model, CAD, computational-design, or robotics development. It therefore fails the publication's lead-topic relevance and novelty tests.\",\"piece_ids\":[]}\n{\"item_id\":\"feed:/resources/supply-chain/oil-refinery/\",\"version_id\":\"03628944819a5390db2f0c3005cb2bcb336118528a4d5c646d7dfe25c82500ea\",\"decision\":\"reject\",\"reason\":\"The item is a fresh but general guide to refinery processes, not evidence about AI for 3D modeling, CAD, geometry, simulation, or robotics. Its process descriptions do not establish a new capability that the audience could build on or verify, so it fails relevance and novelty.\",\"piece_ids\":[]}\n{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://blog.cadalyst.com/cadmanagement/autodesk-university-2026-what-the-announcements-mean-for-cad-teams\",\"version_id\":\"e991bf7bc957baf20c6e947cff7b9a0d159ef7a1b8b9d5f500f7e1c95d47573b\",\"decision\":\"accept\",\"reason\":\"This is fresh CAD-industry reporting with a dated account of Autodesk University and a useful distinction between currently available Assistant features, beta or Tech Preview features, and 2027 previews. Its external checks support availability claims through Autodesk's announcement and Fusion release notes, while the reported live demonstrations remain demonstrations rather than evidence of CAD accuracy, manufacturability, productivity, or production readiness. It adds a commercial CAD perspective, though the accepted set still lacks direct practitioner/customer experience.\",\"piece_ids\":[\"01\",\"02\",\"03\"]}\n{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats\",\"version_id\":\"08b1c2cdebb5796d733d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5\",\"decision\":\"accept\",\"reason\":\"This dated survey is directly relevant to 3D modeling, parametric CAD, and robotics, and explicitly applies the core distinction between editable outputs, benchmark scores, and untested tolerances, manufacturability, and physical transfer. Its evidence is mixed: it reports 1,646 posts across 707 cases but says only 132 cases have runnable code, so most claims are demonstrations rather than independently reproduced results. The transparent limitations and benchmark-versus-harness framing make it useful for scrutiny and implications, while its underlying vendor demonstrations should not be counted as independent corroboration.\",\"piece_ids\":[\"01\",\"02\",\"03\"]}\n{\"item_id\":\"podcast:173f6c2b-e1b8-4de0-b7b7-4b15658d52e6\",\"version_id\":\"da6be0ed10587e503de7706e1ad94cd3d0409d0c4664a004a631d6f27c500a73\",\"decision\":\"reject\",\"reason\":\"The timestamped podcast concerns narrative intelligence, coaching, and human identity in AI rather than 3D modeling, CAD, or intelligent systems acting in physical space. It offers personal perspectives rather than a checkable physical-AI capability or evaluation, so it fails relevance.\",\"piece_ids\":[]}\n{\"item_id\":\"podcast:509842f7-f592-432c-b01a-ad26a2ae1e31\",\"version_id\":\"122e3cc3de2a41fbd9f0de08a089614db628a34697f00a720e1d3abf5b405dca\",\"decision\":\"reject\",\"reason\":\"This fresh episode discusses education in an AI-driven world, with only incidental reference to mechanical engineering. It provides no relevant 3D/CAD or physical-AI development, evidence, or engineering workflow implication, and therefore fails relevance.\",\"piece_ids\":[]}\n{\"item_id\":\"podcast:b836fa1b-9246-43d5-8cbb-cf1446760a6d\",\"version_id\":\"a0beee0f49195b6f0b08b75af793b6f6acaf56a159b58771b1c5d8f6c294b282\",\"decision\":\"defer\",\"reason\":\"The NVIDIA guest interview is relevant robotics context, especially its discussion at timestamps 00:23:52–00:24:25 of simulation from observations and actions, but it is principally a vendor perspective rather than a disclosed evaluation. The supplied automatic transcript does not provide a verified model release, settings, task suite, code, or independent physical-robot check; those primary artifacts and audio verification are needed before its capability claims can be assessed.\",\"piece_ids\":[]}\n{\"item_id\":\"podcast:https://physicalaipodcast.co/episodes/008\",\"version_id\":\"1cb4eefeb597341d29e650ad7c122c9eea10b1aa146aef9c4a6214acdf2c2983\",\"decision\":\"defer\",\"reason\":\"This is promising supporting robotics context because it foregrounds evaluation conditions: at 00:03:29–00:04:26 it describes a blinded, three-attempt-per-task evaluation on another party's arm and distinguishes simulation rankings from real-arm evidence. However, the named model, evaluator, scores, and video evidence appear only in an automatic transcript and are not independently supplied or checked; the primary release, evaluation protocol, results, and video must be verified before accepting the reported numbers. The commissioning company also won the evaluation, an incentive that should be disclosed rather than treated as disqualifying.\",\"piece_ids\":[]}\n{\"item_id\":\"podcast:https://physicalaipodcast.co/episodes/009\",\"version_id\":\"13aaf6bfc525bce0b9a1887162b0c0c3d4dd024b9bafd1c223a5bc48bd063692\",\"decision\":\"defer\",\"reason\":\"The episode is relevant supporting robotics context, particularly its claim at 00:05:24–00:07:35 that an explicit world-action approach generalizes better than a latent approach, while noting simulation-heavy evidence and one held-out real-robot task. But the numerical results, paper identity, code-and-weights claim, and autonomy figures are automatic-transcript material without linked primary artifacts or audio verification. A primary paper, released code and weights, task protocol, and the underlying intervention footage would be needed to judge credibility per claim.\",\"piece_ids\":[]}"}}
./runs/20261016T042241540057Z/logs/editorial-select_items-sh-53a6cdaacebf40a8bff584213d7c5a70-attempt-1.events.jsonl:4:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"item_id\":\"feed:/resources/materials/physical-vapor-deposition/\",\"version_id\":\"fcf9079ad45657e09903d78c29d7b8ae301b4348d9c2d777777b5d1e831378a0\",\"decision\":\"reject\",\"reason\":\"Although fresh and manufacturing-relevant, this is a general materials-process explainer rather than evidence about frontier AI for 3D modeling, CAD, or intelligent physical systems. Its coating-property figures are presented without an identified experimental check, and it supplies no AI-enabled geometry, verification, or simulation development.\",\"piece_ids\":[]}\n{\"item_id\":\"feed:/resources/materials/recrystallization-of-metals/\",\"version_id\":\"ded55799bfd57b9e786dbfccb038115b95d469ba2c6a470457c0869c6e17bd6c\",\"decision\":\"reject\",\"reason\":\"This fresh metallurgy overview is outside the publication's lead topics: it reports conventional material science rather than an AI capability for CAD, 3D modeling, or robotics. The excerpt also provides no new, checkable development or stated experimental conditions that would connect recrystallization knowledge to model-assisted engineering decisions.\",\"piece_ids\":[]}\n{\"item_id\":\"feed:/resources/supply-chain/oil-refinery/\",\"version_id\":\"03628944819a5390db2f0c3005cb2bcb336118528a4d5c646d7dfe25c82500ea\",\"decision\":\"reject\",\"reason\":\"The item is a fresh but generic refinery-process guide, with no frontier-model, CAD, 3D-asset, simulation, or robotics development. Its operating figures are not tied to a disclosed check relevant to the editorial core test, so it cannot support a decision about intelligent systems in physical space.\",\"piece_ids\":[]}\n{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://blog.cadalyst.com/cadmanagement/autodesk-university-2026-what-the-announcements-mean-for-cad-teams\",\"version_id\":\"e991bf7bc957baf20c6e947cff7b9a0d159ef7a1b8b9d5f500f7e1c95d47573b\",\"decision\":\"accept\",\"reason\":\"This is fresh, directly relevant CAD-industry reporting with citable passages distinguishing demonstrated workflows from beta, Tech Preview, and planned releases. The conference demonstrations support only that Autodesk showed an assistant identifying a substitute part and preparing a change order, plus connected design workflows; they do not independently establish production reliability, and the article explicitly preserves that availability distinction. As a trade report on one vendor event it is not independent corroboration of Autodesk capability claims, but it is useful commercial perspective alongside the research evidence.\",\"piece_ids\":[\"01\",\"02\",\"03\"]}\n{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://cableteque.com/blog/build-bom-from-wire-harness-drawing\",\"version_id\":\"1eafb0dcff5f6f5b8c341b6abe613a9a77ce26e7c0fc4eef73af6790f2ae7fe5\",\"decision\":\"defer\",\"reason\":\"The workflow is relevant as a CAD-drawing-to-structured-BOM claim, and the passage says estimators review flagged exceptions rather than every line. However, Cableteque is the claimant and the cited KCM result gives no source corpus, extraction accuracy, error categories, review time, attempt count, or independent evaluation; 18 hours to 45 minutes is therefore a vendor-reported case result, not verified performance. A test on revision-controlled customer drawing packages, with line-item recall and compatibility-error results after human review, would make this checkable.\",\"piece_ids\":[]}\n{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://cableteque.com/blog/does-cableteque-quote-extract-boms-automatically-from-customer-pdf-drawings\",\"version_id\":\"6d917d47e8d4b52b9fe4f0290327adf5dea0f67fa26266abe25051ad74adc0af\",\"decision\":\"defer\",\"reason\":\"This is relevant commercial evidence about extracting manufacturing BOMs from PDF drawings, and it usefully specifies that users should compare extracted line items with the source PDF. But the claimed two-minute workflow and up-to-96% savings are Cableteque's own numbers, with no disclosed document set, error rate, attempt count, or independent check. It also repeats the same underlying product claim as the other Cableteque items, so it cannot serve as corroboration; a reproducible evaluation is needed.\",\"piece_ids\":[]}\n{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://cableteque.com/blog/losing-money-on-cable-assembly-quotes\",\"version_id\":\"33abdbe4f75624efed189d40297ade0c2f12f250f578435d87954b3c34d4693e\",\"decision\":\"reject\",\"reason\":\"This is a fresh restatement of Cableteque's drawing-to-BOM, compatibility, pricing, and labor-automation claims rather than a distinct development. Its Resco turnaround result is a vendor-selected customer report without disclosed conditions or independent verification, and the same publisher's related coverage is not corroboration. It adds no checkable evidence beyond the deferred product claims.\",\"piece_ids\":[]}\n{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://cableteque.com/blog/retiring-estimator-quoting-knowledge\",\"version_id\":\"7684d3f81b48f399e04d30dd15d5c9125d3b3466ed41c07228ed6dc7b4631cc7\",\"decision\":\"reject\",\"reason\":\"The practitioner problem—capturing manufacturing-estimator judgment—is relevant, but this item largely restates the same Cableteque automation claims and vendor-reported performance as the other items from the same publisher. The cited survey and Derrick Lang result lack accessible methodology, task conditions, and independent verification in the supplied passage. It therefore does not add novel, checkable evidence for the series.\",\"piece_ids\":[]}\n{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://cableteque.com/news/ai-wire-harness-quoting-europe\",\"version_id\":\"3f0b6128cabb2c363faca5fff7b5511c50c36083101892f525858da4428f081b\",\"decision\":\"defer\",\"reason\":\"The European expansion is a potentially relevant AI-native startup development, but this is the company's own announcement and it does not independently check that European hosting, distributor integrations, or customer use are operating as described. The passage itself says ISO 27001 certification is only targeted for December 2026, so it cannot support a present-tense security claim. Named customer-access evidence, a demonstrable EU deployment, and confirmed certification status would resolve the uncertainty.\",\"piece_ids\":[]}\n{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats\",\"version_id\":\"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5\",\"decision\":\"accept\",\"reason\":\"This fresh, highly relevant survey directly addresses editable 3D scenes, parametric CAD, and simulation-to-hardware robotics, while stating what its checks observed: public code, videos, documentation, and benchmark results across 707 cases. Its own limitations are decision-useful: only 132 cases include runnable code, CAD benchmark success does not verify tolerances or manufacturability, and physical controllers were not tested under withheld conditions. It provides a research perspective that complements the commercial Autodesk report, though customer and working-engineer experience remains comparatively absent.\",\"piece_ids\":[\"01\",\"02\",\"03\"]}"}}
