./runs/20261015T202519414242Z/logs/editorial-create_briefs-sh-revision-02-d3691d80a851416b9419456bdba2edcb-attempt-1.events.jsonl:4:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"piece_id\":\"02\",\"title\":\"CAD Agents Can Draft More Than They Can Yet Prove\",\"question\":\"Which claims withstand scrutiny, and where are the evidence or interpretations in tension?\",\"argument\":\"The strongest current evidence supports frontier models as drafting aids for editable CAD artifacts under defined software harnesses, not as proof that outputs meet tolerance, manufacturability, or production-approval requirements. Autodesk’s announcements indicate a move toward agentic, connected CAD workflows, while NVIDIA’s process treats conversion of robot CAD into a simulation asset as the start of validation rather than its conclusion. The tension is between claims supported by different checks: benchmark completion, announced availability, and selected simulation tests establish different things.\",\"synthesis\":\"The MIT-CDFG survey separates native-CAD benchmark results from untested physical-part requirements; Cadalyst separates Autodesk capabilities available now from previews and work still in development; and NVIDIA describes distinct visual, physics, and simulation checks after asset conversion. Connecting these sources shows that the appropriate unit of scrutiny is the model-plus-toolchain workflow and its explicit checks. A benchmarked CAD artifact, an announced connected workflow, and a simulated grasp test can each be useful evidence, but none by itself establishes toleranced manufacturability or reliable physical transfer.\",\"limitations\":\"This provisional brief meets the three-independent-publisher requirement but cannot satisfy the startup-watch requirement because the accepted evidence contains no AI-native startup with a checkable claim; Autodesk and NVIDIA are established vendors. The accepted set also contains no podcast evidence, so the series-wide podcast requirement remains unmet. NVIDIA’s video describes a validation procedure, not pass rates, failure rates, or physical-transfer results.\",\"claims\":[{\"claim_id\":\"02-c1\",\"statement\":\"The MIT-CDFG survey reports that, on 100 native FreeCAD benchmark tasks, leading models average 85% and fully solve about 45 tasks, while tolerance specifications and manufacturability have not been tested on physical parts.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats\",\"version_id\":\"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"On 100 FreeCAD benchmark tasks, the leading models average 85%, up from 70% for GPT-5.6 Sol, and fully solve about 45\\n(Parametric CAD Bench 2026)\\n. Tolerance specifications and manufacturability have not been tested on physical parts.\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c2\",\"statement\":\"Cadalyst reports that Autodesk’s announced Fusion automation and connected-client capabilities have mixed availability, with several larger capabilities still announced or in development.\",\"type\":\"reported_fact\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://blog.cadalyst.com/cadmanagement/autodesk-university-2026-what-the-announcements-mean-for-cad-teams\",\"version_id\":\"e991bf7bc957baf20c6e947cff7b9a0d159ef7a1b8b9d5f500f7e1c95d47573b\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"Mixed. Autodesk Assistant capabilities are already appearing in manufacturing products, while several of the larger automation and connected-client capabilities remain announced or in development.\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c3\",\"statement\":\"NVIDIA’s video describes checking a converted robot asset’s visual properties, scale, parts, and appearance against references before later physics and simulation checks.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw\",\"version_id\":\"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0004\"}},{\"item_id\":\"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw\",\"version_id\":\"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0005\"}},{\"item_id\":\"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw\",\"version_id\":\"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0006\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c4\",\"statement\":\"CAD-agent claims should be limited to the artifact or behavior actually checked, because the accepted evidence does not show that CAD benchmark scores, announced workflows, or selected simulation grasp tests establish manufacturability or physical transfer.\",\"type\":\"inference\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats\",\"version_id\":\"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"Tolerance specifications and manufacturability have not been tested on physical parts.\"}},{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://blog.cadalyst.com/cadmanagement/autodesk-university-2026-what-the-announcements-mean-for-cad-teams\",\"version_id\":\"e991bf7bc957baf20c6e947cff7b9a0d159ef7a1b8b9d5f500f7e1c95d47573b\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"Some of what Autodesk demonstrated is ready to use today. Other capabilities remain in Tech Preview, beta, or the “coming soon” category.\"}},{\"item_id\":\"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw\",\"version_id\":\"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0006\"}}]}]}"}}
