./runs/20261015T202519414242Z/logs/editorial-create_briefs-sh-draft-02-ec268395b8a84443b9c402968af06293-attempt-1.events.jsonl:4:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"piece_id\":\"02\",\"title\":\"CAD Agents Can Draft More Than They Can Yet Prove\",\"question\":\"Which claims withstand scrutiny, and where are the evidence or interpretations in tension?\",\"argument\":\"The strongest current evidence supports frontier models as drafting aids for editable CAD artifacts under defined software harnesses, not as proof that their outputs meet tolerance, manufacturability, or production-approval requirements. Autodesk’s announcements signal a move toward agentic, connected CAD workflows, while NVIDIA’s workflow shows that simulation-ready robotics assets require staged validation after conversion. The tension is not between opposing results: it is between claims made at different levels of checking.\",\"synthesis\":\"The MIT-CDFG survey separates benchmarked native-CAD performance from untested physical requirements; Cadalyst distinguishes Autodesk features available now from previews, beta, and planned releases; and NVIDIA describes separate visual, physics, and simulation checks for converted robot CAD. Together, these sources indicate that the relevant unit of evaluation is the workflow and its checks—not a model output or a vendor demonstration alone. A valid parametric solid, a planned connected workflow, or a completed grasp test each establishes something useful, but none alone establishes manufacturability or dependable physical deployment.\",\"limitations\":\"This provisional brief has exactly three independent publishers, but no accepted podcast evidence, so the series-wide podcast requirement is unmet. The accepted set also contains no clearly evidenced AI-native startup to test; Autodesk and NVIDIA are established vendors, so this draft cannot satisfy the startup-watch requirement without inventing a startup example. NVIDIA’s evidence is an automatic transcript and describes a prescribed validation process rather than reported pass rates or physical transfer results.\",\"claims\":[{\"claim_id\":\"02-c1\",\"statement\":\"The MIT-CDFG survey reports that, on 100 native FreeCAD benchmark tasks, leading models average 85% and fully solve about 45 tasks, while tolerance specifications and manufacturability have not been tested on physical parts.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats\",\"version_id\":\"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"On 100 FreeCAD benchmark tasks, the leading models average 85%, up from 70% for GPT-5.6 Sol, and fully solve about 45\\n(Parametric CAD Bench 2026)\\n. Tolerance specifications and manufacturability have not been tested on physical parts.\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c2\",\"statement\":\"Cadalyst reports that Autodesk’s announced Fusion automation and connected-client capabilities have mixed availability, with several larger capabilities still announced or in development.\",\"type\":\"reported_fact\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://blog.cadalyst.com/cadmanagement/autodesk-university-2026-what-the-announcements-mean-for-cad-teams\",\"version_id\":\"e991bf7bc957baf20c6e947cff7b9a0d159ef7a1b8b9d5f500f7e1c95d47573b\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"Mixed. Autodesk Assistant capabilities are already appearing in manufacturing products, while several of the larger automation and connected-client capabilities remain announced or in development.\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c3\",\"statement\":\"NVIDIA’s video describes checking a converted robot asset’s visual properties, scale, parts, and appearance against references before later physics and simulation checks.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw\",\"version_id\":\"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0004\"}},{\"item_id\":\"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw\",\"version_id\":\"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0005\"}},{\"item_id\":\"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw\",\"version_id\":\"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0006\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"02-c4\",\"statement\":\"A CAD-agent capability claim should be limited to the artifact or behavior actually checked, because the accepted evidence does not show that CAD benchmark scores, announced workflows, or selected simulation grasp tests establish manufacturability or physical transfer.\",\"type\":\"inference\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats\",\"version_id\":\"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"Tolerance specifications and manufacturability have not been tested on physical parts.\"}},{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://blog.cadalyst.com/cadmanagement/autodesk-university-2026-what-the-announcements-mean-for-cad-teams\",\"version_id\":\"e991bf7bc957baf20c6e947cff7b9a0d159ef7a1b8b9d5f500f7e1c95d47573b\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"Some of what Autodesk demonstrated is ready to use today. Other capabilities remain in Tech Preview, beta, or the “coming soon” category.\"}},{\"item_id\":\"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw\",\"version_id\":\"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0006\"}}]}]}"}}
