./runs/20261015T202519414242Z/logs/editorial-create_briefs-sh-draft-02-8374b5b1d1474f219f5d58606da883d9-attempt-1.events.jsonl:4:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"piece_id\":\"02\",\"title\":\"CAD Checks Are Becoming More Explicit; Proof Is Still Narrow\",\"question\":\"Which claims withstand scrutiny, and where are the evidence or interpretations in tension?\",\"argument\":\"The strongest current evidence supports frontier models as first-draft tools for editable 3D scenes and parametric CAD, not as substitutes for engineering validation. CAD benchmarks and simulation checks establish narrower properties—task completion, solid validity, visual correspondence, configured joints, and simulated grasps—while tolerances, manufacturability, and physical transfer remain untested or undisclosed. For technical leaders, the key distinction is not whether an agent produced an artifact, but which properties its workflow actually checked.\",\"synthesis\":\"The MIT-CDFG survey supplies the broad CAD evidence and explicitly identifies missing tolerance and manufacturability tests; NVIDIA’s walkthrough shows the more concrete but still simulation-bounded checks a CAD-to-robotics workflow can run; the podcast’s RobotWorld discussion illustrates why completion claims need execution-based graders and why simulated outcomes should not be generalized to physical deployment. Together, they indicate that verification is becoming part of the agentic geometry workflow, but the verification layer must be matched to the decision: kernel or simulation checks can qualify a draft for further work, not certify a manufactured part or real-world robot behavior.\",\"limitations\":\"The survey is an authors’ synthesis of public evidence, with runnable code in only 132 of 707 cases, while NVIDIA does not disclose an error rate, reproducible configuration, or physical-transfer result. The podcast transcript is automatic and its RobotWorld figures require 