./runs/20261015T202519414242Z/logs/editorial-create_briefs-sh-revision-03-b6bd808b572e4e199b19a269c8ea76c8-attempt-1.events.jsonl:4:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"piece_id\":\"03\",\"title\":\"Build the verification layer; wait on autonomous CAD decisions\",\"question\":\"What do these developments imply for the audience, and what should they watch next?\",\"argument\":\"Engineering leaders should build durable CAD data, review, and verification workflows now, while treating agentic design and simulation features as systems that prepare work for domain-specific checks rather than as engineering authorities. The near-term opportunity is making parametric models, configuration logic, approvals, and simulation-ready asset checks inspectable enough that emerging agents can participate without silently becoming the authority. This recommendation follows from evidence that current model outputs can serve as first drafts, while tolerance, manufacturability, and safe unsupervised operation remain unestablished.\",\"synthesis\":\"The evidence supports a common operating model across CAD and robotics: automation can carry work across connected tools, but the confidence warranted by an output is limited by the check performed at each handoff. Autodesk’s report makes permissions, initiation authority, review, and approval explicit governance questions; PTC’s tutorial shows one face-based query workflow surviving one upstream edit; NVIDIA specifies visual, scale, physics, and grasp checks for a converted robot asset. The MIT survey supplies the boundary condition: editable CAD drafts and validated solids do not establish tolerance conformance, manufacturability, or safe unsupervised operation. Together, these sources support building native-data and verification infrastructure, selectively buying workflow features whose outputs and checks can be inspected, and waiting to delegate engineering approval or physical-transfer claims to agents.\",\"limitations\":\"The accepted evidence is dominated by vendor, platform, and survey sources rather than independent customer deployments or reproducible comparative trials. NVIDIA’s workflow establishes the checks its process prescribes, not reliable hardware transfer; Autodesk’s announced and previewed capabilities are not evidence of production performance; and the PTC tutorial demonstrates one edit path rather than comparative robustness. No accepted evidence identifies an AI-native startup suitable for the required startup-watch build, buy, or wait role. The accepted set also contains no podcast evidence, so this provisional brief cannot satisfy the series’ cross-format requirement.\",\"claims\":[{\"claim_id\":\"03-c1\",\"statement\":\"The Cadalyst report says that some Autodesk University capabilities are available today, while others are in beta, Tech Preview, or a longer-term direction not ready for immediate production use.\",\"type\":\"reported_fact\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://blog.cadalyst.com/cadmanagement/autodesk-university-2026-what-the-announcements-mean-for-cad-teams\",\"version_id\":\"e991bf7bc957baf20c6e947cff7b9a0d159ef7a1b8b9d5f500f7e1c95d47573b\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"Some of the capabilities Autodesk discussed at AU are available today. Some are in beta or Tech Preview. Still others represent a longer-term direction rather than something companies should expect to put into production immediately.\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"03-c2\",\"statement\":\"Teams should establish reviewable CAD governance before allowing agents to prepare or initiate consequential changes, because the cited evidence identifies data access, action authority, review, approval, and unmet engineering requirements as remaining control points.\",\"type\":\"inference\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://blog.cadalyst.com/cadmanagement/autodesk-university-2026-what-the-announcements-mean-for-cad-teams\",\"version_id\":\"e991bf7bc957baf20c6e947cff7b9a0d159ef7a1b8b9d5f500f7e1c95d47573b\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"Organizations still need to determine which project information an AI tool can access, who can initiate changes, how generated results are reviewed, and what constitutes an approved deliverable.\"}},{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats\",\"version_id\":\"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"These outputs are now reliable enough to serve as first drafts, but the record does not yet show that they meet engineering requirements such as tolerances, manufacturability or safe unsupervised operation.\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"03-c3\",\"statement\":\"PTC’s Onshape release says compatible configuration inputs can automatically propagate to reference Part Studios, assemblies, and Variable Studios.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"youtube:7i5XHQozfQI\",\"version_id\":\"582b91bc01228116ecd2d1c461bae76ac4715fc7ed39fa319cf08c96874fdc2e\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0003\"}},{\"item_id\":\"youtube:7i5XHQozfQI\",\"version_id\":\"582b91bc01228116ecd2d1c461bae76ac4715fc7ed39fa319cf08c96874fdc2e\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0004\"}},{\"item_id\":\"youtube:7i5XHQozfQI\",\"version_id\":\"582b91bc01228116ecd2d1c461bae76ac4715fc7ed39fa319cf08c96874fdc2e\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0005\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"03-c4\",\"statement\":\"In PTC’s demonstrated example, adding an upstream slot leaves the ruled surface updated with drafted faces and no errors because the query follows the face rather than individually selected edges.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"youtube:jLndwlduyzc\",\"version_id\":\"a3209c32c4153b9ba8b68c0b0d0fc493c4e67807292a91e91e6e5a2c9c2cd938\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0029\"}},{\"item_id\":\"youtube:jLndwlduyzc\",\"version_id\":\"a3209c32c4153b9ba8b68c0b0d0fc493c4e67807292a91e91e6e5a2c9c2cd938\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0030\"}},{\"item_id\":\"youtube:jLndwlduyzc\",\"version_id\":\"a3209c32c4153b9ba8b68c0b0d0fc493c4e67807292a91e91e6e5a2c9c2cd938\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0032\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"03-c5\",\"statement\":\"NVIDIA’s CAD-to-simulation workflow calls for checking visual properties, imported scale and parts, joint and collision configuration, selected foundation checks, and a grasp test before a full Isaac Sim sequence.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw\",\"version_id\":\"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0004\"}},{\"item_id\":\"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw\",\"version_id\":\"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0005\"}},{\"item_id\":\"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw\",\"version_id\":\"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0006\"}},{\"item_id\":\"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw\",\"version_id\":\"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0007\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"03-c6\",\"statement\":\"The MIT survey states that no benchmark yet tests CAD tolerance specifications or manufacturability on physical parts.\",\"type\":\"reported_fact\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats\",\"version_id\":\"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"Tolerance specifications and manufacturability have not been tested on physical parts.\"}}]}]}"}}
./runs/20261015T202519414242Z/items.jsonl:4:{"item_id":"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats","kind":"feed","media_url":null,"published_at":"2026-09-22T00:00:00Z","publisher_id":"mit-cdfg","retrieved_at":"2026-10-15T20:25:19Z","segments_path":null,"source_id":"mit-cdfg-survey","text_path":"data/assets/feed_https_mit-cdfg.github.io_Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics_view-stats/08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5.txt","title":"On the Opportunities and Risks of Frontier Models for 3D Modeling, Computational Design and Robotics","url":"https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/","version_id":"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5"}
./runs/20261015T202519414242Z/items.jsonl:18:{"item_id":"podcast:b836fa1b-9246-43d5-8cbb-cf1446760a6d","source_id":"practical-ai","publisher_id":"changelog-media","kind":"podcast","title":"Open models and the future of Physical AI with NVIDIA","url":"https://pscrb.fm/rss/p/dts.podtrac.com/redirect.mp3/media.transistor.fm/217445d3/e1eb0b6a.mp3","published_at":"2026-10-01T09:00:00Z","retrieved_at":"2026-10-15T20:25:21Z","media_url":"https://pscrb.fm/rss/p/dts.podtrac.com/redirect.mp3/media.transistor.fm/217445d3/e1eb0b6a.mp3","version_id":"a0beee0f49195b6f0b08b75af793b6f6acaf56a159b58771b1c5d8f6c294b282","text_path":"data/assets/podcast_b836fa1b-9246-43d5-8cbb-cf1446760a6d/a0beee0f49195b6f0b08b75af793b6f6acaf56a159b58771b1c5d8f6c294b282.txt","segments_path":"data/assets/podcast_b836fa1b-9246-43d5-8cbb-cf1446760a6d/a0beee0f49195b6f0b08b75af793b6f6acaf56a159b58771b1c5d8f6c294b282.segments.json"}
./runs/20261015T202519414242Z/logs/editorial-create_briefs-sh-revision-03-672a3d2fe03e42b0a70f1d173c09efca-attempt-1.events.jsonl:4:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"piece_id\":\"03\",\"title\":\"From CAD Features to Dependable Workflows: What Teams Should Watch Next\",\"question\":\"What do these developments imply for the audience, and what should they watch next?\",\"argument\":\"The near-term signal is a set of released CAD workflow features and vendor previews, not evidence that connected AI workflows improve engineering outcomes. Engineering leaders should treat AI-assisted design, CAD-to-simulation conversion, and agentic workflow previews as governed pilots, requiring explicit checks for geometry, physical properties, manufacturability, and approval authority before downstream use.\",\"synthesis\":\"Taken together, the sources support an evaluation model rather than a demonstrated productivity outcome: preserve structured CAD artifacts, identify the specific check at each handoff, and keep approvals explicit. Onshape describes configuration propagation and assembly center-of-mass display; Autodesk coverage separates currently available functions from previews and planned work; NVIDIA describes distinct visual, physics, and simulation checks for CAD-to-simulation conversion. The MIT survey establishes the boundary condition: editable solids and benchmark completion do not establish tolerance conformance or manufacturability. The added analysis is therefore to treat connected workflow claims as a chain of separately validated handoffs, rather than as proof supplied by a fluent model or a successful import.\",\"limitations\":\"This evidence does not include independent customer deployments, productivity measurements, physical-part inspections, or a direct test that the shown CAD-to-simulation workflow transfers accurately to hardware. Autodesk’s connected and agentic capabilities span available, beta, Tech Preview, development, and planned statuses; the retained evidence does not establish their production readiness or operational benefit.\",\"claims\":[{\"claim_id\":\"03-c1\",\"statement\":\"Onshape’s October release says it can automatically propagate configuration inputs to reference Part Studios, assemblies, and Variable Studios, a workflow change for parametrically driven CAD models.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"youtube:7i5XHQozfQI\",\"version_id\":\"582b91bc01228116ecd2d1c461bae76ac4715fc7ed39fa319cf08c96874fdc2e\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0003\"}},{\"item_id\":\"youtube:7i5XHQozfQI\",\"version_id\":\"582b91bc01228116ecd2d1c461bae76ac4715fc7ed39fa319cf08c96874fdc2e\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0004\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"03-c2\",\"statement\":\"Onshape’s release says persistent center-of-mass display is available in assemblies for individual components or an entire assembly.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"youtube:7i5XHQozfQI\",\"version_id\":\"582b91bc01228116ecd2d1c461bae76ac4715fc7ed39fa319cf08c96874fdc2e\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0007\"}},{\"item_id\":\"youtube:7i5XHQozfQI\",\"version_id\":\"582b91bc01228116ecd2d1c461bae76ac4715fc7ed39fa319cf08c96874fdc2e\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0008\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"03-c3\",\"statement\":\"Autodesk University coverage distinguishes capabilities available today from beta, Tech Preview, development, and planned releases.\",\"type\":\"reported_fact\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://blog.cadalyst.com/cadmanagement/autodesk-university-2026-what-the-announcements-mean-for-cad-teams\",\"version_id\":\"e991bf7bc957baf20c6e947cff7b9a0d159ef7a1b8b9d5f500f7e1c95d47573b\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"Some of the capabilities Autodesk discussed at AU are available today. Some are in beta or Tech Preview. Still others represent a longer-term direction rather than something companies should expect to put into production immediately.\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"03-c4\",\"statement\":\"A CAD-to-simulation asset workflow should be assessed through separate visual, physics, and simulation checks rather than treating import into a simulator as proof of physical correctness.\",\"type\":\"inference\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw\",\"version_id\":\"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0004\"}},{\"item_id\":\"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw\",\"version_id\":\"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0005\"}},{\"item_id\":\"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw\",\"version_id\":\"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"segment\",\"segment_id\":\"s0006\"}},{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats\",\"version_id\":\"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"Tolerance specifications and manufacturability have not been tested on physical parts.\"}}]},{\"claim_id\":\"03-c5\",\"statement\":\"The MIT survey reports that frontier models can produce editable parametric parts and assemblies, but says the record does not show that outputs meet tolerance, manufacturability, or safe-unsupervised-operation requirements.\",\"type\":\"source_claim\",\"evidence\":[{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats\",\"version_id\":\"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"Models build parametric parts and assemblies, and solid models of up to 511 bodies, that remain editable in native CAD tools\"}},{\"item_id\":\"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats\",\"version_id\":\"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5\",\"locator\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"quote\":\"These outputs are now reliable enough to serve as first drafts, but the record does not yet show that they meet engineering requirements such as tolerances, manufacturability or safe unsupervised operation.\"}}]}]}"}}
./runs/20261015T202519414242Z/candidates/podcasts.jsonl:3:{"item_id":"podcast:b836fa1b-9246-43d5-8cbb-cf1446760a6d","source_id":"practical-ai","publisher_id":"changelog-media","kind":"podcast","title":"Open models and the future of Physical AI with NVIDIA","url":"https://pscrb.fm/rss/p/dts.podtrac.com/redirect.mp3/media.transistor.fm/217445d3/e1eb0b6a.mp3","published_at":"2026-10-01T09:00:00Z","retrieved_at":"2026-10-15T20:25:21Z","media_url":"https://pscrb.fm/rss/p/dts.podtrac.com/redirect.mp3/media.transistor.fm/217445d3/e1eb0b6a.mp3"}
./runs/20261015T202519414242Z/decisions.jsonl:5:{"item_id":"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats","version_id":"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5","decision":"accept","reason":"This recent, dated survey directly addresses frontier models in 3D modeling, computational design, and robotics, and its excerpt supplies a citable scope: 1,646 public posts and accompanying evaluations across 707 cases. It is useful research-oriented evidence because it explicitly separates public demonstrations and vendor reports from stronger evaluation, but its broad conclusions about verified solids and benchmark improvement must be traced to the underlying checks, settings, and graders before being treated as settled. It adds a research perspective alongside commercial CAD coverage, though customer and working-engineer perspectives remain missing.","piece_ids":["01","02","03"]}
./runs/20261015T202519414242Z/decisions.jsonl:8:{"item_id":"podcast:b836fa1b-9246-43d5-8cbb-cf1446760a6d","version_id":"a0beee0f49195b6f0b08b75af793b6f6acaf56a159b58771b1c5d8f6c294b282","decision":"defer","reason":"The title and dated introduction suggest potentially relevant physical-AI context, including open models, but the supplied passage only introduces an NVIDIA guest and gives no capability claim, experimental conditions, result, or timestamped audio passage to check. Defer pending a substantive cited segment and primary release or evaluation evidence showing what was tested, by whom, and under what conditions.","piece_ids":[]}

{"piece_id":"01","title":"CAD AI Moves Into Engineering Workflows, While Verification Stays Separate","question":"What changed, and which developments matter most to this audience?","argument":"The important change is that AI is entering native CAD and simulation workflows through custom-feature generation, visual search, simulation setup, and drawing-to-CAD conversion. The evidence supports supervised use for first drafts, retrieval, and simulation preparation—not treating generated geometry as production-ready by default. For engineering leaders, the immediate decision is where these tools can accelerate bounded work while existing tolerance, manufacturability, and validation gates remain in place.","synthesis":"The evidence connects a market shift with a verification boundary. Onshape and SimScale describe AI interfaces embedded in CAD workflows, while Engineering.com independently tested a narrow drawing-to-CAD case; together, they indicate that the near-term change is more assisted entry into engineering systems, not demonstrated autonomous engineering. NVIDIA’s simulation-asset workflow adds explicit checks for visual, physics, and grasp properties, while the MIT-CDFG survey identifies tolerances and manufacturability as still untested; the analytical implication is that teams should align each release gate to the specific property actually checked.","limitations":"Engineering.com’s independent CAD test covered two successful speed-modeling drawings and one failed multibody drawing, not tolerance-critical shop drawings or production parts. Most remaining CAD and simulation evidence is vendor reporting about capabilities or workflows; representative internal test sets, disclosed failure rates, and downstream manufacturing checks are still absent.","claims":[{"claim_id":"01-c1","statement":"Engineering.com reported that Backflip passed mass checks for two drawing-to-CAD STEP outputs after import into Fusion.","type":"reported_fact","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563","version_id":"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"I can verify that Backflip got it exactly correct."}},{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563","version_id":"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"once again, Backflip aced the test."}}]},{"claim_id":"01-c2","statement":"Engineering.com reported that Backflip created only one of seven bodies for its multibody wire-shelf drawing test.","type":"reported_fact","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563","version_id":"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"it only created one of the seven bodies"}}]},{"claim_id":"01-c3","statement":"Onshape says its FeatureScript MCP Server lets AI create, test, debug, and refine custom CAD functionality from plain-language requests.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/featurescript-mcp-server-use-cases-text-code-cad","version_id":"9656a0a35f95ac0e0adb6ba1530e2759342633cbcecba8de9948699a032ab5a8","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"create, test, debug, and refine custom CAD functionality"}}]},{"claim_id":"01-c4","statement":"Onshape says AI Descriptive Image Search generates searchable descriptions from model thumbnails.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/ai-generated-thumbnail-description-search","version_id":"b2755a997c3cdda71421bbdd40ee9721d353cbc20ed2f1f80e91169757b9bbd2","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"generates searchable descriptions from model thumbnails"}}]},{"claim_id":"01-c5","statement":"Onshape says its AI Descriptive Image Search currently applies only to public documents.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/ai-generated-thumbnail-description-search","version_id":"b2755a997c3cdda71421bbdd40ee9721d353cbc20ed2f1f80e91169757b9bbd2","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"public documents only"}}]},{"claim_id":"01-c6","statement":"SimScale says its Onshape agent can propose a simulation setup, mesh and run physics, and report results in the Onshape environment.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151329","version_id":"a5a6e64fd4f2a87b5b45be5afd0c01d19fd3ee1e8b8f12be9e7e49964e03d82b","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"propose an appropriate setup, mesh and run the physics"}},{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151329","version_id":"a5a6e64fd4f2a87b5b45be5afd0c01d19fd3ee1e8b8f12be9e7e49964e03d82b","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"report the results back in the same Onshape environment"}}]},{"claim_id":"01-c7","statement":"The MIT-CDFG survey says current frontier-model outputs can serve as first drafts but do not yet show tolerance conformance or manufacturability.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats","version_id":"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"reliable enough to serve as first drafts"}},{"item_id":"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats","version_id":"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"Tolerance specifications and manufacturability have not been tested"}}]},{"claim_id":"01-c8","statement":"NVIDIA says its CAD-to-simulation workflow checks imported scale, parts, appearance, joints, collision geometry, physical properties, and a grasp test before testing a full sequence in Isaac Sim.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0004"}},{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0005"}},{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0006"}},{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0007"}}]},{"claim_id":"01-c9","statement":"The Physical AI Podcast reported that five general models left 63 of 84 robot tasks unsolved in RobotWorld.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"podcast:https://physicalaipodcast.co/episodes/010","version_id":"85cb287a2441351d8480acecc45d2ae3d6c8d1e3d180eee941a34d7bcda762d1","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0005"}}]},{"claim_id":"01-c10","statement":"Because NVIDIA’s workflow checks simulation-asset properties while the MIT-CDFG survey identifies manufacturability as untested, passing those simulation checks should not be interpreted as demonstrating manufacturability.","type":"inference","evidence":[{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0006"}},{"item_id":"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats","version_id":"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"manufacturability have not been tested"}}]}]}
{"piece_id":"02","title":"The Check Is the Capability","question":"Which claims withstand scrutiny, and where are the evidence or interpretations in tension?","argument":"CAD agents now merit attention as first-draft and workflow tools, but the evidence supports narrower conclusions than many product narratives imply. Engineering.com found two Backflip drawing conversions that passed a specified mass check, while the same test found a multibody failure and incomplete feature reconstruction. The practical question is not whether an agent produced or executed something, but whether the check matches the downstream engineering decision.","synthesis":"The MIT survey distinguishes editable parametric output from proof of tolerance conformance and manufacturability. Engineering.com’s Backflip test makes that distinction concrete: a material-and-mass check can verify a selected property of a converted STEP model, while a separate multibody task failed and an add-in reconstruction was incomplete. NVIDIA’s sim-ready workflow adds another bounded check set—visual reference comparison, configured physical properties, and grasp tests—so the editorial conclusion is that teams should procure and deploy agentic CAD against explicitly tested properties, not a general claim of engineering readiness.","limitations":"Backflip’s evaluation used a small, curated set of speed-modeling drawings, not production shop drawings, and its mass checks do not establish feature-level correctness, tolerance conformance, or manufacturability. Commercial workflow claims from SimScale, Onshape, and NVIDIA do not provide independent error rates, disclosed tolerance-critical test suites, or physical-transfer outcomes. The podcast’s reported robotics evaluation is supporting context only; its transcript-derived numerical details were not independently audio-verified here.","claims":[{"claim_id":"02-c1","statement":"The MIT survey says frontier models build parametric parts and assemblies, while tolerance specifications and manufacturability have not been tested on physical parts.","type":"reported_fact","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats","version_id":"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"Models build parametric parts and assemblies"}},{"item_id":"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats","version_id":"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"Tolerance specifications and manufacturability have not been tested"}}]},{"claim_id":"02-c2","statement":"Engineering.com reports importing a Backflip STEP output into Fusion, applying material, and checking its mass.","type":"reported_fact","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563","version_id":"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"import it into Fusion, apply the material, and check the mass"}}]},{"claim_id":"02-c3","statement":"Engineering.com says that Backflip got the first drawing-to-CAD mass check exactly correct.","type":"reported_fact","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563","version_id":"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"I can verify that Backflip got it exactly correct."}}]},{"claim_id":"02-c4","statement":"Engineering.com reports that Backflip created only one of seven bodies on a multibody drawing-to-CAD task.","type":"reported_fact","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563","version_id":"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"it only created one of the seven bodies"}}]},{"claim_id":"02-c5","statement":"Engineering.com reports that Backflip’s Fusion add-in only partially recreated geometry for one tested part.","type":"reported_fact","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563","version_id":"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"the add-in only partially recreated the geometry"}}]},{"claim_id":"02-c6","statement":"A selected-output mass check supports mass agreement for that test, not tolerance conformance or manufacturability.","type":"inference","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563","version_id":"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"apply the material and check the mass"}},{"item_id":"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats","version_id":"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"confirming conformance to tolerances and manufacturability requires further evaluation"}}]},{"claim_id":"02-c7","statement":"SimScale says its Engineering AI Agent can propose a setup, mesh a CAD model, run physics, and report results in Onshape.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151329","version_id":"a5a6e64fd4f2a87b5b45be5afd0c01d19fd3ee1e8b8f12be9e7e49964e03d82b","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"propose an appropriate setup, mesh and run the physics"}}]},{"claim_id":"02-c8","statement":"Onshape says its FeatureScript MCP Server lets LLMs help create, test, debug, and refine custom CAD functionality.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/featurescript-mcp-server-use-cases-text-code-cad","version_id":"9656a0a35f95ac0e0adb6ba1530e2759342633cbcecba8de9948699a032ab5a8","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"create, test, debug, and refine custom CAD functionality"}}]},{"claim_id":"02-c9","statement":"NVIDIA says its CAD-to-simulation workflow checks imported robot scale, parts, and appearance against references.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0004"}}]},{"claim_id":"02-c10","statement":"NVIDIA says its workflow configures joints, collision geometry, and physical properties for arms and grippers.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0005"}}]},{"claim_id":"02-c11","statement":"NVIDIA says its workflow runs sim-ready foundation checks and a grasp test, then fixes issues and reruns them.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0006"}}]},{"claim_id":"02-c12","statement":"The Physical AI Podcast reports that the operator in a Black Forest Labs-commissioned evaluation was blind to which model was driving.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"podcast:https://physicalaipodcast.co/episodes/008","version_id":"1cb4eefeb597341d29e650ad7c122c9eea10b1aa146aef9c4a6214acdf2c2983","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0044"}}]}]}
{"piece_id":"03","title":"Adopt the Workflow, Not the Unchecked Output","question":"What do these developments imply for the audience, and what should they watch next?","argument":"The immediate opportunity is to use AI for first-draft CAD, reusable feature development, model search, and simulation preparation while keeping release decisions behind checks matched to engineering risk. Teams should watch for evidence that generated geometry passes tolerance, manufacturability, and downstream validation—not merely that it forms a valid model or completes a simulation workflow. The most useful next signal will be disclosed, representative evaluations that separate model output from the CAD, simulation, and review systems around it.","synthesis":"The evidence distinguishes useful workflow automation from verified engineering outcome. MIT’s survey finds editable CAD outputs promising but identifies missing physical-part tolerance and manufacturability tests; Engineering.com’s Backflip trial shows a narrow mass check can validate a specific output while another drawing fails a basic multibody requirement. Meanwhile, Onshape and NVIDIA describe product pathways for text-to-CAD features and CAD-to-simulation preparation, making the operational question for engineering leaders which independent release checks remain mandatory after AI has accelerated upstream work.","limitations":"Evidence for production readiness remains incomplete. Engineering.com tested a small, curated set of Backflip drawings, while the Onshape and NVIDIA descriptions are vendor claims; no accepted evidence provides an independently replicated comparison of AI-generated CAD features or simulation-ready assets against production tolerances, manufacturability, or real-world transfer. The robotics evidence is supporting context, not evidence that CAD workflows transfer physically.","claims":[{"claim_id":"03-c1","statement":"The MIT survey says its record does not show that frontier-model outputs meet engineering requirements such as tolerances or manufacturability.","type":"reported_fact","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats","version_id":"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"does not yet show that they meet engineering requirements"}}]},{"claim_id":"03-c2","statement":"Engineering.com reported that Backflip created only one body from a seven-body drawing-to-CAD test.","type":"reported_fact","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563","version_id":"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"it only created one of the seven bodies"}}]},{"claim_id":"03-c3","statement":"Engineering.com reported that its Backflip drawing-to-CAD test imported a STEP file into Fusion, applied material, and checked mass.","type":"reported_fact","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563","version_id":"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"import it into Fusion, apply the material, and check the mass"}}]},{"claim_id":"03-c4","statement":"Onshape says its FeatureScript MCP Server enables engineers to have AI help create, test, debug, and refine custom CAD functionality from plain-language requests.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/featurescript-mcp-server-use-cases-text-code-cad","version_id":"9656a0a35f95ac0e0adb6ba1530e2759342633cbcecba8de9948699a032ab5a8","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"create, test, debug, and refine custom CAD functionality"}}]},{"claim_id":"03-c5","statement":"NVIDIA describes checking an imported robot’s scale, parts, and appearance against references in its CAD-to-simulation workflow.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0004"}}]},{"claim_id":"03-c6","statement":"NVIDIA describes using grasp tests and selected simulation-readiness checks before running a full sequence in Isaac Sim.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0006"}},{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0007"}}]},{"claim_id":"03-c7","statement":"The Physical AI Podcast reports that five general models left 63 of 84 robot tasks unsolved in RobotWorld, whose program checked whether each job was completed.","type":"source_claim","evidence":[{"item_id":"podcast:https://physicalaipodcast.co/episodes/010","version_id":"85cb287a2441351d8480acecc45d2ae3d6c8d1e3d180eee941a34d7bcda762d1","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0005"}},{"item_id":"podcast:https://physicalaipodcast.co/episodes/010","version_id":"85cb287a2441351d8480acecc45d2ae3d6c8d1e3d180eee941a34d7bcda762d1","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0036"}}]},{"claim_id":"03-c8","statement":"Because the survey identifies untested physical-part tolerances and manufacturability while NVIDIA describes simulation-readiness checks, simulation-ready status should not be treated as release-ready manufacturing evidence.","type":"inference","evidence":[{"item_id":"feed:https://mit-cdfg.github.io/Survey-AI-for-3D-modeling-Robotics/#view-stats","version_id":"08b1c2cdebb5796d7332d57e8928784a621aec9f82db91448016eaa53ea51fe5","locator":{"type":"text","quote":"Tolerance specifications and manufacturability have not been tested on physical parts."}},{"item_id":"youtube:H5UaOdIZERw","version_id":"c86fae4c3874c6aa997843fe5a3365bf0a32d21acb0927fbcea32b10e0709a06","locator":{"type":"segment","segment_id":"s0006"}}]}]}
